          8,230,816                 69,702,169  89% \n30 Bihar             19,461,013               102,457,579  86% \n31 Manipur                3,008,546                    2,508,663  83% \n32 Arunachal \nPradesh \n               1,528,296                    1,241,708  81% \n33 Jammu Kashmir              13,635,010                 10,540,421  77% \n34 Nagaland                2,189,297                    1,290,359  59% \n35 Meghalaya                3,276,323                       927,238  28% \n36 Assam              34,586,234                    4,264,361  12% \n TOTAL         1,335,140,907             1,230,811,923 92.2% \n  \n \n*As per data provided by State  \n\n \n******* \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |688", "t": "Special Category Status", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 688 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2019 \n  \nSPECIAL CATEGORY STATUS \n  \n688. SHRI  M. MURALIMOHAN: \n  \n Will the Minister of PLANNING be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the number of States that have been granted Special Category Status (SCS); \n \n(b) the criterion followed for granting SCS; and \n \n(c)  the reasons for not granting special status to the State of Andhra Pradesh? \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \n                                                     (RAO INDERJIT SINGH)   \n                      \n(a) & (b) Eleven States granted Special Category Status are Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, \nHimachal Pr adesh, Jammu & Kashmir, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim, \nTripura and Uttarakhand.  Special Category Status for plan assistance has been granted in the \npast by the National Development Council (NDC) to some States that are characterized by a \nnumber of features necessitating special consideration.   These features include: (i) hilly and \ndifficult terrain (ii) low population density and / or sizeable share of tribal population (iii) \nstrategic location along borders with neighbouring countries (iv)  economic and infrastructural \nbackwardness and (v) non -viable nature of state finances. Special Category Status has been \ngranted based on an integrated consideration of these criteria.  \n  \n   (c)   The Fourteenth Finance Commission recommended the transfer to the states by \ndevolution and grants -in-aid for the period 2015 -20 which was accepted by the Central \nGovernment. The Fourteenth Finance Commission (FFC) in its recommendations has not ma de \nany distinction between General Category States and Special Category States in the horizontal \ndistribution of shareable taxes amongst the States. As per the recommendations of the \nFourteenth Finance Commission,  the States’ share of Central taxes has incr eased from 32% \nearlier to 42% for the period 2015 -2020. Also, post-devolution revenue deficit grants have \nbeen provided to States where devolution alone could not cover the assessed gap. The \n14th Finance Commission has recommended Revenue Deficit Grant of Rs.22,112 crore to \nAndhra Pradesh for the period 2015-20.  \n       \n        Even though special category status has not been conferred to the state of Andhra \nPradesh, the Central Government has agreed to give a special assistance measure for \nGovernment of An dhra Pradesh for five years, which would make up for the additional \nCentral share the State might have received during these years i.e, 2015 -16 to 2019 -20. \nThis will be in the form of Central Government funding for externally aided projects for the \nstate of Andhra Pradesh signed and disbursed during these years. \n---- \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |689", "t": "Meetings of MPs with Concerned GMs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 689 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2019 \n \nMEETINGS OF MPs WITH CONCERNED GMs \n \n689.  SHRI KONAKALLA NARAYANA RAO: \n \n   Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken note of the fact that the \nadvisory Committee meetings being held with concerned \nrailway GMs and Members of Parliament has displayed \ndissatisfaction among MPs as none of the advices have been \ntaken into consideration, while taking up railway projects in \ntheir respective States;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n \n(c) the steps being taken by the Government to achieve fruitful \nresults of such meetings in future? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI RAJEN GOHAIN) \n(a) to ( c): The suggestions given by Hon’ble Members of \nParliament (MPs) during the meetings of Divisional Committee \nheld with Zonal  Railways are duly noted and considered while \nplanning/executing projects which are subject to feasibility, \nneed and availability of funds. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |69", "t": "Procedure for Recovery of Outstanding Amount", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.69 (9th Position) \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \nSanitation Scheme \n *69. SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \nSHRI SATAV RAJEEV: \n Will the Minister of DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION  be pleased to \nstate: \n (a) the total amount of grant provided to Below Poverty Line (BPL) and Above \nPoverty Line (APL) families under sanitation scheme in rural areas during each of the \nlast three years and the current year, separately, State/UT-wise; \n (b) the rate of per BPL and APL family assistance provided during the above period; \n (c) whether the rate of assistance per family has ever been reduced by the \nGovernment, if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government to \nincrease the assistance provided under the scheme; \n (d) whether any financial assistance has been received from private companies and \nPSUs under the Corporate Social Responsibility activities in the sanitation sector in \nrural areas being run by the Government and if so, the details thereof; and \n (e) whether the Government is contemplating to invite Public Private Partnership \n(PPP) in the sanitation sector and also in supply of drinking water in rural areas, if so, \nthe details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER FOR DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n(SUSHREE UMA BHARATI) \n \n(a) to (e)   A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement referred to in the reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No.69 due for \nreply on 07.02.2019. \n (a) & (b)       Under Swachh Bharat Mission (Gramin) [SBM(G)], financial incentive \nof Rs.12,000 [in the ratio of 60:40 between the Centre and States (90:10 in case of \nNorth-Eastern and special category States)] is provided for the construction of \nIndividual Household Latrine (I HHL) to all Below Poverty Line (BPL) households \nand identified Above Poverty Line (APL) households (i.e. all SCs/STs, small and \nmarginal farmers, landless labourers with homestead, physically handicapped and \nwomen-headed households) in rural areas.  As per the data reported by the States/UTs \non the Integrated Management Information System of SBM(G), a total amount of \nRs.69782.75 crore has been provided to the BPL and APL beneficiaries for \nconstructions of IHHLs during the last three years and the current ye ar (up to \n05.02.2019).  Year-wise and State-wise details are given at Annexure-1. \n (c)    No, Madam. \n(d) and (e) SBM(G) guidelines have provisions to facilitate the utilization of \nCorporate Social Responsibility (CSR) funds in rural sanitation activities. \nStates/concerned Districts can use these guidelines as a base to develop their own \nprocedure to attract/receive and utilize CSR funds.  Several rounds of meeting were \nheld with various corporate groups, both in public and private sector, to harness their \nskills and resources in implementing SBM at Central level.  Further, a Swachh Bharat \nKosh (SBK) has been established under the Ministry of Finance to channelize \ncorporate and individual contributions for SBM. These contributions are covered \nunder Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) under the Companies Act.  Rs.638.25 \ncrore have been released under SBK. \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-1 \nStatement referred in part (a) of the reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No.69 due for reply \non 07-02-2019 \nYear-wise   and State/UT-wise amounts provided to BPL and APL beneficiaries \nfor   constructions of IHHLs during the last three years and the current year (Rs.   in \nCrore) \nState/UT 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19    \n (up to 05.02.19) \nA & N Islands 0.00 0.20 5.71 11.66 \nAndhra Pradesh 420.59 827.32 2199.27 519.71 \nArunachal Pradesh 22.56 80.54 79.49 5.37 \nAssam 543.47 623.80 759.46 841.36 \nBihar 419.04 246.62 234.54 3304.11 \nChandigarh 0.00 0.00 0.00 0.00 \nChhattisgarh 334.52 630.49 831.84 370.93 \nD & N Haveli 0.00 0.00 0.00 20.74 \nDaman & Diu 0.00 0.00 0.00 2.17 \nGoa 6.44 0.00 7.70 20.20 \nGujarat 747.23 1021.71 632.16 119.80 \nHaryana 73.92 10.71 1.28 0.75 \nHimachal Pradesh 68.20 77.05 0.15 0.31 \nJammu & Kashmir 73.18 57.39 169.62 193.04 \nJharkhand 337.24 682.54 900.44 687.39 \nKarnataka 587.55 712.23 1366.42 908.28 \nKerala 12.74 224.00 1.22 0.02 \nLakshadweep 0.00 0.00 0.00 0.00 \nMadhya Pradesh 1051.90 1842.35 1219.86 1285.93 \nMaharashtra 829.65 1007.29 1324.37 944.72 \nManipur 58.98 5.04 4.95 32.62 \nMeghalaya 47.33 41.08 83.24 17.94 \nMizoram 6.60 3.46 28.93 0.85 \nNagaland 27.14 45.65 2.96 61.27 \nOdisha 1584.48 1434.73 741.79 1353.59 \nPuducherry 0.00 2.65 0.99 33.27 \nPunjab 91.17 145.29 77.76 50.12 \nRajasthan 1615.29 1760.50 1385.41 475.60 \nSikkim 4.45 0.00 0.00 0.00 \nTamil Nadu 729.11 862.21 1263.08 328.28 \nTelangana 207.36 280.00 421.03 628.82 \nTripura 56.27 20.60 15.22 107.00 \nUttar Pradesh 742.85 1855.16 4365.74 10178.30 \nUttarakhand 76.27 161.01 175.60 46.54 \nWest Bengal 1224.99 1339.52 669.76 260.41 \n Total 12,000.53 16,001.15 18,969.98 22,811.09 \n  \n\n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |690", "t": "e-Ticket Online", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 690 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2019 \n \nE-TICKET ONLINE \n \n690. SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL: \n  \n         Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Railways has launched a scheme allowing a person to   \nget an insurance cover of upto Rs. 10 lakh on booking a train ticket  \nonline by paying less than on rupee; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the said facility is reserved for passengers of the Railways \nwho book e-Ticket and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government has received any suggestions to \nincorporate other passengers who book ticket through traditional \nmethods; and \n \n(e) if so, the response of the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n ( SHRI RAJEN GOHAIN ) \n \n(a) to (c):  Yes, Madam. An Optional Travel Insurance Scheme with a \ncover of upto `10 lakh was launched w.e.f 01.09.2016 for the \nConfirmed/RAC railway passengers who book online e -ticket through \nofficial website of Indian Railway Catering & Tourism Corporation \n(IRCTC) which is a wholly owned undertaking of Ministry of Railways.  \nContd..2/- \n \n\n-2- \nW.e.f 01.10.2018, the premium for the scheme is ` 0.49/- \n(inclusive of all taxes) per passenger per trip.  \nUnder the scheme, in case of death/injury of reserved passengers \ndue to train accident/untoward incidents as defined under section 123 \nread with Sections 124 and 124A of the Railways Act, 1989, the sum \nassured is paid to the victim/family or legal heir of the victim as the \ncase may be.  \nThe insurance coverage is valid from the actual departure of \ntrain from the originating station to actual arrival of train at the \ndestination station including ‘proces s of entraining’ and ‘process of \ndetraining’ the train. \nThe s um assured given to the victim/family or legal heir of the \nvictim is as follows:-  \n(i) In case of Death- ` 10 lakh,                  \n(ii) Permanent Total Disability - ` 10 Lakh,    \n(iii) Permanent Partial Disability  upto- ` 7.5 Lakh,  \n      (iv) Hospitalization Expenses for Injury – ` 2 Lakh,  \n      (v)Transportation of mortal remains – ` 10 Thousand. \n        \nPresently, IRCTC is providing this insurance facility through                          \n3 Insurance Companies namely (i) M/s Bharti Axa General Insurance \nCo. Ltd., (ii) Bajaj Allianz General Insurance Co. Ltd., & (iii) Shriram \nGeneral Insurance Co. Ltd. \n \n(d) and (e): No, Madam. Optional Travel Insurance Scheme is being run \non a pilot basis w.e. f. 01.09.2016 and the scheme has been extended \nup to 31.08.2019. The scheme is available only to Confirmed/RAC \nrailway passengers who book their online e -ticket through Indian \nRailway Catering and Tourism Corporation (IRCTC). \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |691", "t": "Fund Crunch in MGNREGS", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 691 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \n FUND CRUNCH IN MGNREGS  \n \n691.  SHRI TEJ PRATAP SINGH YADAV:  \n SHRI L.R. SHIVARAME GOWDA:  \n SHRIMATI ANJU BALA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Emplo yment Guarantee Scheme \n(MGNREGS) is facing a severe fund crunch and faces difficulties in meeting the demand \nfor work and paying wages on time;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether 250 law-makers and activists have writt en to the Prime Minister raising concerns \nover implementation of the MGNREGS;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the action taken  thereon;  \n(e) whether there is urgent need for strengthening of MGNREGS amid a situation of distress \nacross rural India with massive unemployment and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(f) the steps taken/being taken by the Government t o improve the living conditions of rural \nworkers across the country?  \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV) \n \n(a)& (b): The Ministry releases funds to the State/ UT based on the basis of the agreed to \nLabour Budget, opening balance, pending liabilities  of the previous year for the financial year, if \nany, and overall performance. Fund release to State s/UTs is a continuous process and Central \nGovernment is committed in making funds available k eeping in view the demand for work. \nDuring FY 2018-19 (as on 04.02.2019), Rs 55,687.47 crore has been released under \nthe Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guaran tee Scheme (MGNREGS) to \nStates/UTs . The Ministry seeks additional amount for MGNREGA as and when required for \nmeeting the demand for work on the ground. \n \n(c)& (d): Ministry of Rural Development has been re ceived various representation/ \nreferences regarding implementation of the MGNREGS from Prime Minister’s Office. \nSince the \nresponsibility of implementation of MGNREGA is vest ed with the State Governments, all \n\nrepresentation/ references are forwarded to the concerned State Governments fo r taking \nappropriate action including investigation, as per law.  \n \n(e)& (f): The Government has taken steps to strengt hen implementation of MGNREGA \nfrom time to time. These include \n:- \n \n(i) Implementation of National electronic Fund Manageme nt System (Ne-FMS) and \nfocus on Aadhaar Based Payment system, streamlining  fund flow mechanism, and \nmonitoring of timely payment of wages to workers. \n \n(ii) Training of Barefoot Technicians, capacity building  of MGNREGA functionaries \nsuch as State Technical Resource Team / District Te chnical Resource Team / Block \nTechnical Resource Team on Mission Water Conservati on works, training on Geo-\nMGNREGA, training to Finance officers of States on preparation of fund proposals \netc. \n \n(iii) Framework for strengthening of monitoring mechanism  developed and advisories \nissued for inspection of MGNREGA works by State, Di strict and Block level \nofficials and National Level Monitors. \n \n(iv) The Ministry has initiated Job Cards (JCs) verifica tion of MGNREGA workers to \nweed out the bogus and duplicates JCs. \n \n(v) Auditing Standards have been issued and States have  been advised to establish \nindependent Social Audit Units, conduct Social Audi t as per Audit of Scheme Rules, \ntraining of Village Resource Persons for conducting Social Audit etc. \n \n(vi) A path breaking initiative for improved planning, e ffective monitoring and greater \ntransparency with the use space technology for geo- tagging of MGNREGA assets \nhas also been launched. \n \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |692", "t": "DDU-GKY", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 692 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \n DDU-GKY  \n \n692.  SHRI B. SENGUTTUVAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to impart Skill  Development Training to the rural \nyouth living in Naxalinfested and Terrorism-affecte d areas under the Deen Dayal \nUpadhyaya Grameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDU-GKY) and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) the total number of such persons proposed to be  given skill development training in the \ncurrent financial year under the DDU-GKY, State-wise;  \n(c) whether the Government also proposes to impart training in Information Technology for \nthe skill development of rural youth; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof?  \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV) \n \n(a): The Government is implementing Deen Dayal Upad hyaya Grameen Kaushalya Yojana \n(DDU-GKY), a placement linked skill development pro gram for rural poor youth under National \nRural Livelihoods Mission (NRLM) all over the count ry. Under DDU-GKY a special initiative \ncalled Roshni has been launched that takes into acc ount the peculiar situations in left wing \nextremist affected 27 districts. Under this initiat ive skill development shall be mandatory \nresidential course with 40% coverage to women candi dates. Roshni is oriented towards \naddressing the infrastructure, education, and health deficiencies in these areas, and leveraging the \navailability of natural resources and traditional s kills and knowledge. A special scheme Himayat \nis being operated for Jammu and Kashmir under DDU-G KY. This is a 100% centrally funded \nscheme implemented with the help of the State Gover nment by a dedicated Himayat Mission \nManagement Unit located in both Srinagar and Jammu.   In addition to areas covered under \nRoshni and Himayat, trainings under DDU-GKY are provided in other affected areas.  \n  \n(b): The State-wise number of candidates proposed t o be trained under DDU-GKY in the year \n2018-19 is  given below:- \n  \nSl.No \n State  Target 2018-19  \n1 Andhra Pradesh 6728  \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 524  \n3 Assam 9112  \n\n4 Bihar 17236  \n5 Chhattisgarh 8354  \n6 Gujarat 3482  \n7 Haryana 4565  \n8 Himachal Pradesh 1741  \n9 Jammu and Kashmir 14414  \n10  Jharkhand 9247  \n11  Karnataka 13716  \n12  Kerala 8264  \n13  Madhya Pradesh 10672  \n14  Maharashtra 6773  \n15  Manipur 1221  \n16  Meghalaya 744  \n17  Mizoram 542  \n18  Nagaland 804  \n19  Odisha 20153  \n20  Punjab 1741  \n21  Rajasthan 13349  \n22  Sikkim 186  \n23  Tamil Nadu 7477  \n24  Telangana 5491  \n25  Tripura 2934  \n26  Uttar Pradesh 21417  \n27  Uttarakhand 580  \n28  West Bengal 8533  \n  Total 200000  \n  \n   \n(c)& (d): Every candidate undergoing training under  DDU-GKY is given mandatory basic \ntraining in Information Technology for 80 Hours. Du ring the year 2018-19 (upto   31st January, \n2019)  1,76,630 candidates have been provided training.  \n \n \n***** \n  \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |693", "t": "Names of Women Farmers in Land Records", "head": "GOVERNMEN丁OF INDIA\nMIN:STRY OF RURAL DE∨ELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF LAND RESOURCES\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUES丁10N N0 693\nTO BE ANSVVERED ON 07 02 2019\nNames of Women Farrners in Land Records\n693.SHRI GUTHA SUKENDER REDDY:\nWi∥the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pieased to state:\n(a)Whether the Covernment proposes to revise the guidelines to enable women\nfarmers'names in the land records so as to enabie them to get proper benefits of\nCentra∥y sponsored schemesi and\n(b)if SO,the deta∥s thereof?\nANSWER\nM:N:STER OF STATE FOR RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a)&(b):   As per Entry No 18 and 45 ofthe List∥(State List)of the seventh\nSchedule to the Constitution of india, the subieCt regarding land and its\nmanagement, including the maintenance of land records,fa∥s within the,uriSdiCtion\nof States\nThe Digitai lndia Land Records Modernisation Programme fr7rer a″a prOvides\nfor digitisation of ava∥able land records"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |694", "t": "Details of Various Schemes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.694 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nDetails of Various Schemes \n \n†694. SHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \n  SHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO:  \n SHRI P.P. CHAUHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of the schemes launched by the Ministry of Youth \nAffairs and Sports since the year 2014 till date along with the details \nof the targets set and achieved thereunder, year-wise;  \n \n(b) the details of the proposals received from various States/UTs along \nwith the proposals approved by the Ministry under these schemes \nduring the said period, State/UT-wise;  \n \n(c) the details of the funds allocated and utilized for implementation of \nvarious schemes under the Ministry during the said period, \nState/UT/year-wise; and  \n \n(d) whether the Government has set any time -limit for completion/ \nimplementation of pending schemes and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[Col. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)] \n \n(a) & (b):  \n \nDepartment of Youth Affairs:  \n \nIn pursuance to the 2014 -15 Budget announcement, a new Central \nSector Scheme, namely, ‘National Young Leaders Programme (NYLP)’  \n\nwas formulated with a view to develop leadership qualities among the \nyouth to enable them to realize their full potential and in the process, \nto contribute to the nation-building process.  After obtaining necessary \napprovals, the Scheme was launched in December, 2014.  The National \nYoung Leaders Programme is a Central Sector Scheme . The Scheme \nhas the following five components: \n \n1. Neighbourhood Youth Parliament (NYP). \n2. Youth for Development Programme (YFDP). \n3. National Young Leaders Awards (NYLA). \n4. National Youth Advisory Council (NYAC). \n5.   National Youth Development Fund (NYDF) \n \nThe year -wise achievement of the National Young Leaders \nProgramme/Scheme are at Annexure I. \n \nDepartment of Sports :  \nDetails of the schemes launched by Department of Sports since the \nyear 2014 is given at Annexure-II.  \nSports is a State subject. Promotion and development of sports is \nprimarily the responsibility of the State Governments and National \nSports Fede rations (NSFs) concerned. The Central Government \ncomplements / supplements the efforts of the States in this regard. \nThe sports Infrastructure projects under above schemes are demand -\ndriven and proposals, as and when received from States, if complete in \nall respects are sanctioned subject to technical feasibility and \navailability of funds. Therefore, no state -wise fund allocation is made \nunder these schemes.  State/UT-wise details of sports infrastructure \nprojects sanctioned under RGKA and Khelo India schemes are given at \nAnnexure – III. \n(c) The Schemes/Programmes of the Ministry of Youth Affairs and \nSports are being implemented for encouragement and development of \nyouth and sports in the country as a whole. Hence, the funds are not \nallocated state -wise.  The details of the funds allocated and utilized \nfor implementation of various schemes under the Department of Youth \nAffairs and Department of Sports  during the said period, are  at \nAnnexure-IV and V respectively: \n\n(d) No Scheme of the Department of Youth Affairs is pending.  In so \nfar as  the Department of Sports, the completion of construction of \nmain campus of National Sports University may take around 2 years. \nThe completion of NCSSR and NCSC is set from 2017 -18 to 2019 -20. \nThe rest of the schemes are on going schemes whose implementation \nis a continuous process and regarding the approved projects the time \nfor completion of the projects totally depends upon the concerned \nbeneficiary. \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO  IN REPLY TO PART (A)  & (B) OF THE  LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION N O.694 RAISED BY SHRI  NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA , \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO AND SHRI P.P. CHAUHAN REGARDING DETAILS \nOF VARIOUS SCHEMES. \nAchievements of National Young Leaders Programme (NYLP) during the year 2014-15 \n \nS. \nNo. \nName of Zone Block Level Neighbourhood \nYouth Parliament \nYouth for Development Programme \n(IEC-Publicity & Media Campaign) \nNo. of \nprogrammes \norganized \nParticipants No. of \nprogrammes \norganized \nParticipants \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 206 16480 14 2100 \n2.  Telangana 101 8080 9 1350 \n3.  Arunachal Pradesh  73 5840 12 1800 \n4.  Assam  236 18880 24 3600 \n5.  Bihar  534 42720 38 5700 \n6.  Chhattisgarh  132 10560 16 2400 \n7.  Delhi 13 1040 9 1350 \n8.  Gujarat, Daman & \nDiu and Dadra \nNagar Haveli \n84 6720 1 150 \n9.  Haryana 110 8800 18 2700 \n10.  Himachal Pradesh  78 6240 12 1800 \n11.  Jammu & Kashmir  103 8240 14 2100 \n12.  Jharkhand  169 13520 14 2100 \n13.  Karnataka  157 12560 25 3750 \n14.  Kerala & \nLakshadweep \n149 \n11920 \n16 \n2400 \n15.  Madhya Pradesh  266 21280 48 7200 \n16.  Maharashtra & Goa  344 27520 36 5400 \n17.  Manipur  43 3440 10 1500 \n18.  Meghalaya 32 2560 7 1050 \n1"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |695", "t": "National Rural Livelihoods Mission", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 695 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \n NATIONAL RURAL LIVELIHOODS MISSION  \n \n695.  SHRI ANOOP MISHRA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether schemes like National Rural Livelihoods  Mission (NRLM) and National Health \nMission (NHM) have been utilised and proved to be s uccessful in improving the basic \namenities like health conditions etc. of the people;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) the number of people benefited from the same, S tate/UT-wise including Madhya \nPradesh?  \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV) \n \n(a): Yes, Sir. \n(b)& (c): The National Health Mission (NHM) aims fo r attainment of universal access to \nequitable, affordable and quality health care servi ces, accountable and responsive to people’s \nneeds, with effective inter-sectoral convergent act ion to address the wider social determinants of \nhealth. NHM has been successful in accelerating the  decline of Maternal Mortality Ratio \n(MMR), Under 5 Mortality Rate (U5MR), Infant Mortal ity Rate (IMR) and Total Fertility Rate \n(TFR). It has also achieved many of the disease con trol targets. The key achievements of NHM \nare as follows. \n \n(a) Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) has reduced from 42 in 2012 to 34 in 2016 (SRS). \n(b) Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) has reduced from 167 in 2011-13 to 130 in 2014-16 (SRS). \n(c) Total Fertility Rate (TFR) has reduced from 2.9 in 2005 to 2.3 in 2016 (SRS). \n(d) In respect of Tuberculosis (TB), the incidence of T B reduced from 234/ lakh population in \n2012 to 204/lakh population in 2017. Mortality also  reduced to from 38/ lakh population \nduring 2012 to 31/ lakh population in 2017. \n(e) In respect of Leprosy, the Prevalence rate of Lepro sy was less than 1/10000 population in \n542 districts in 2014 which increased to 571 Districts in 2018. \n \n \n\n(f) In respect of Malaria, the Annual Malaria Incidence was 0.89 in 2014-15 and 0.17 in 2018-19 \n(up to Sept 2018). \n \n(g) In respect of Microfilaria(mf), as of December 2018 , out of 256 Lymphatic Filariasis(LF) \nendemic districts, 99 districts have achieved <1% m f prevalence in all 8 sites (4 sentinel + 4 \nRandom sites) and stopped MDA after validation by T ransmission Assessment Survey, \nwhich is the preliminary goal of elimination.  Rest  157 districts are yet to achieve <1% mf \nprevalence. \n(h) In respect of Kala Azar, till 2018 (November), 583 (92%) blocks reported <1 case of Kala-\nazar /10,000 population at block level. \nThe State -wise details of the number of Sub Centre (SCs), Primary Health Centre ( PHCs ) and \nCommunity Health. Centre ( CHCs ) are given as Annexure I. \nThe Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana-National Rural Livel ihoods Mission (DAY-NRLM) was \nlaunched in June 2011 with the objective to organiz e all rural poor households in the country and \ncontinuously nurture and support them till they com e out of abject poverty. DAY-NRLM also \npromotes Food, Nutrition, Health and WASH (Water, S anitation and Hygene) interventions \nbased on convergence and partnership  between commu nity institutions and line Departments \npertaining to Health and Hygene. \nUnder convergence activity, nutritious food is bein g provided to the children at Anganwadi \nCenters of ICDS in some of the Sates. Members of SH Gs are also providing hot cooked meals \nunder Mid-Day-Meal scheme in some of the School.  \nFor improved results on nutrition, Ministry of Rura l Development (DAY-NRLM scheme), \nMinistry of Health and Family Welfare ( National He alth Mission) and Ministry of Women and \nChild Development (Integrated Child Development Ser vices (ICDS) Scheme)  have jointly \nissued an advisory on 17 \nth  January 2018 and identified the following key areas and modalities for \nconvergent action. \na) Convergence through Village, health, Sanitation and Nutrition Day (VHSND) \nb) Convergence for Behavioural Change and Communication. \nc) Convergence for Monitoring and Management of Nutritional Status of Women and Children. \nd) Convergence through Common Use of Infrastructure. \ne) Promoting Livelihoods for Women’s Collectives. \nWomen Self Help Groups members under DAY-NRLM have participated in various kinds of \nactivities under Poshan Abhiyan to spread important  massages on nutrition. Overall DAY-\nNRLM has conducted 178,175 activities wherein 3.81 crore members participated as part of \nPoshan Maah . \nState Rural Livelihoods Missions (SRLMs) are also b eing encouraged to develop FNHW \nresource blocks @ two blocks per state in FY-2019-2 0 in order to demonstrate how to bring \nbehavioural change among the SHG members in Health and Nutrition related practices and \ndevelop strong convergence between Community Based Organisations (CBOs) and line \ndepartments. \n\nTo provide on-job technical support to SRLMs and to  develop effective training materials and \naids for supporting i"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |696", "t": "Environment/Social Impact Assessment of National Inland Waterway-1", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 696\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 7IH FEBRUARY, 2019\nENVIRONMENT/ SOCIAL IMPACT ASSESSMENT OF NATIONAL INLAND WATERWAY-1\n696. SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL:\nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state:\nda qlr.cra i*\n(a) whether an environment clearance was obtained for the National lnland WateMay No.1\nand if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n(b) whether a social and an Environment lmpact Assessment of the proposed project was\nconducted and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and\n(c) the details of negative impacts of the said prolect on the ecology of Ganga river or the\nlocal human population inhabiting the surrounding area, if any?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING\n(sHRr PON RADHAKRTSHNAN)\n(a) to (c): The Jal Marg Vikas project (JMVP) is being implemented for augmentation of\nnavigation capacity on the Haldia - Varanasi stretch of National waterway-1 on Ganga-\nBhagirathi-Hooghly River System with technical and financial support of the World Bank. The\nMinistry of Environment, Forests & climate change (MoEF&cc) has concurred with the\ndecision that as per extant legal position, no prior Environment Clearance (EC) is required for\nmaintenance dredging in navigational channel for lnland Waterways inclucling dredging under\nJMVP. The clea ra nces/approva ls and details of public consultation under JMVp are at\nAnnex.\nTo safeguard lhe environment and ecology, lnland Waterways Authority of lndia\n(lWAl) has undertaken Detailed Environment Assessment (EA) for JMVP through renowned\nconsultants as per requirement under the World Bank operational policies. As part of the EA,\nextensive consultations with stakeholders (including Locals and Civil Society Organisations)\nwere carried out by the consultanls in June, July, September, october and November of 2015\nand in February,2016 along the project Corridor. Additionally, the draft cumulative lmpact\nAssessment Report; the Consolidated Environmental lmpact Assessment Report containing\nthe Environment Management Plans (EMps) for maintenance dredging, barge operations, the\n\n-2-\nFarakka Navigational Lock, the multimodal terminals at Sahibganj, Varanasi and Haldia, the\nExecutive Summary, and the Consolidated Social lmpact Assessment-cum-Resettlement\nAction Plan were published on the lnland Waterways Authority of lndia (lWAl) website in May,\n20'16 for information and comments from the public. These reports were finalised on the basis\nof comments received from the public and the World Bank.\nThe National Board for Wildlife (NBWL), MoEF&CC accorded permission for\nmovement and plying of inland vessets through the Turtle Wildlife Sanctuary, Varanasi, Uttar\nPradesh on 12rh June 2017 as no dredging is proposed in the wildlife sanctuary areas such as\nVikramashila Gangetic Dolphin Sanctuary, Bhagalpur and Kashi Turtle Sanctuary, Varanasi.\nlwAl commissioned a consultancy in December,2017 lo carry out a special study\non the effect of navigational activities on the Gangetic Dolphin. The scope of the study on\n'Effect of Navigational Activities on Dolphin in the NW- 1 ' was finalized in a brain-storming\nsession held on 14th October, 2016 at lWAl, Noida with the subject experts from Patna\nUniversity, Titka Manlhi Bhagalpur University, Wildlife lnstitute of lndia, National Mission for\nClean Ganga (NMCG) and also in consultation with the World Bank.\nUnder the JMVP, a Consultant has been engaged by the lWAl for identification of\nRisk Assessment (RA) and preparation of Disaster Management Plan (DMP) for National\nWaterway-1. The objective of consultancy service is to assess the risk associated with\nongoing and proposed cargo and passenger movement and other navigational activities\nincluding maintaining fairway through NW-1 and identifying abetment measures towards\ndeveloping a proposal for integration of IWT related disasters into existing District Disaster\nManagement Plan (DDMP).\n\n(JMVP):\n-3-\nAn nex\nClearances and approvals received\n. Wildlife clearance for the movement of vessels through Kashi Turtle Wildlife Sanctuary,\nVaranasi: The National Board for Wildlife (NBWL), MoEF&CC recommended for movement\nand plying of inland vessels through the Turtle Wildlife Sanctuary, Dislrict Varanasi, Uttar\nPradesh vide letter dated 12rh June, 2017 of UP State Board for Wildlife.\n. CRZ Clearance for the Multimodal Terminal at Haldia: The MoEF&CC granted the CRZ\nclearance for Multimodal Terminal at Haldia on 6th November 2017.\n. Oil Spill Disaster Contingency Plan approved by lndian Coast Guard, Haldia on 22nd\nDecember 20'16.\n. National Ganga River Basin Authority (NGRBA) examined and supported the proposal\nfor construction of New Navigational Lock at Farakka on 28.02.2016\n. The ICAR-Central lnland Fisheries Research lnstitute (ICAR-ClFRl), Barrackpore had\ncarried out a special study on the 'lmpact assessment of coal transportation through barges\nalong the National Waterway-1 (Sagar to Farakka"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |697", "t": "Slow Progress in NH Projects", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 697 \nANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2019 \n  \nSLOW PROGRESS IN NH PROJECTS \n \n697.  SHRI K.R.P. PRABAKARAN: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry is aware that  a number of highway projects are moving  at a snails \npace due to low funding across the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the details of ongoing/pending projects/upgradation work in highways in Tamil Nadu? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to (b) No Madam.     \n \n(c)   Details of the ongoing and delayed projects in the state of Tamil Nadu is as follows :   \n  \nS No Items Nos Length (km) Cost (Cr) \n1 Ongoing Works  77 2400.07 27826.01 \n2 Delayed Works 37 1424.74 9868.58 \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |698", "t": "Film Festivals of Regional Languages", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 698 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019) \n \nFILM FESTIVALS OF REGIONAL LANGUAGES \n698 SHRI S. RAJENDRAN \nSHRI S.R. VIJAYAKUMAR \nSHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA \nKUNWAR HARIBANSH SINGH \nSHRI T. RADHAKRISHNAN \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to \nstate: \na) Whether various regional languages film festivals are organized in the country; \nb) if so, the details of the regional film festivals organized during each of the last \nthree year and the current year, State/UT-wise; \nc) the total expenditure incurred for the said purpos e during the said period, \nState/UT-wise; \nd) whether the Government proposes to provide financial assistance to film -\nmakers whose films are selected in competition section of an International \nFilm Festival; \ne) if so, the details of the financial assistance provided by the Government during \neach of the last three years and the current year, Film-wise; and \nf) the other steps taken /being taken by the Government to promote the regional \nlanguage films in the country as well as at International Level? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nINFORMATION AND BROADCASTING {COL. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE \n(RETD.)} \n \n(a to c) Yes, Madam. The details of the regional language film festivals and the \nfinancial assistance provided by the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting are \nattached as Annexure ‘A’; \n(d & e) Ministry of Information & Broadcasting is providing financia l assistance to \nIndian Films which are selected in various International Film Festivals, namely \nOscars, Toronto, Bussan, Berlin etc. During the last three years financial \nassistance has been provided to two films in 2017 -18, namely ‘Newton’ \n(Rs.6,68,051/-) and ‘Loktak Lairembee’ (Rs.8,70,396/ -),  which were selected in \nthe Berlin International Film Festival. \nContd./- \n \n\nf) Every year, Ministry of Information & Broadcasting organizes National Film \nAwards wherein films from across the country in Hindi/Regional Languages are \nawarded under different categories. \nAlso, every year, films are selected for Indian Panorama section of the \nInternational Film Festival of India (IFFI). From these entries, 47 Films, including \n26 Feature Films and 21 Non -Feature films, are se lected in various Indian \nlanguages.  \n \nIndian Panorama films are screened in IFFI as well as in various Indian & \nInternational Film Festivals and Indian Film Weeks/ festivals abroad. \n \n……………. \n \n  \n\nAnnexure ‘A’ \nFilm Festivals of Regional Language Films \n \n \nS. \nNo \nName of Festival Date State/U.T. Financial \nAssistance \nprovided \nby M/o I&B \n2016 \n1 Indian Panorama Film Festival \nin Patna  \nFebruary, 2016 Bihar -- \n2 Indian Panorama Film Festival \nin Bengaluru \nMarch, 2016 Karnataka 648315 \n3 North-East Film Festival in \nMysore  \nJuly, 2016 Karnataka -- \n4 Screening of 63rd National \nAward winning films in NFAI, \nPune \nMay, 2016 Maharashtra  -- \n5 Indian Panorama Film Festival \nin Shillong  \nAugust, 2016 Meghalaya 808600 \n6 Patriotic Film Festival in \nWardha  \nSeptember –\nOctober, 2016 \nGujarat 300000 \n7 Patriotic Film Festival in Jorhat September –\nOctober, 2016 \nAssam 300000 \n8 Patriotic Film Festival in \nKolhapur  \nDecember \n2016 \nMaharashtra 191772 \n2017 \n1 Indian Panorama Public \nScreening \nJanuary 2017 New Delhi 615919 \n2 3rd Rajasthan In’l Film Festival January 2017 Rajasthan -- \n3 North East Film Festival in \nPune \nJanuary 2017 Maharashtra  4475032 \n4 Pune Int’l Film Festival, Pune  January 2017 Maharashtra  -- \n5 Bhojpuri Film Festival February 2017 New Delhi 678044 \n6 Patriotic Film Festival in \nRohtas  \nFebruary 2017 Bihar 224400 \n7 Patriotic Film Festival, Itanagar  February 2017 Arunachal \nPradesh \n300000 \n8 Patriotic Film Festival Indore, \nIndore  \nFebruary, \n2017 \nMadhya \nPardesh \n163535 \n9 Patriotic Film Festival In \nJharkhand \nMarch, 2017 Jharkhand 300000 \n10 Patriotic Film Festival In \nGuwahati  \nMarch 2017 Assam 300000 \n11 Patriotic Film Festival in Banka  March 2017 Bihar 220471 \n12 Patriotic Film Festival, \nChhattisgarh \nMarch 2017 Chhattisgarh 224000 \n13 Patriotic Film Festival, Uttar \nPradesh \nMarch 2017 Uttar Pradesh 258500 \n14 Patriotic Film Festival in \nGurdaspur \nMarch 2017 Punjab  267440 \n15 International Spiritual Film \nFestival , Varanasi \nMarch 2017 Uttar Pradesh  375000 \n\n16 Mahatma Gandhi Film Festival \nin Motihari  \nApril 2017 Bihar -- \n17 Kashmir World Film Festival. April 2017 Kashmir 1000000 \n18 Patriotic Film Festival in \nCoimbatore  \nMay 2017 New Delhi 300000 \n19 Patriotic Film Festival in \nNalanda  \nJuly 2017 Bihar 300000 \n2018 \n1. 15 Mumbai Int’l film festival in \nMumbai  \nFebruary, \n2018 \nMaharashtra -- \n2. 13th International film festival \nin Thrissur  \nMarch 2018 Kerala -- \n3. Lal Bahadur Shastri National \nAcademy of Administration \nOrganized film festival, \nDeharadun  \nMarch 2018 Uttrakhand -- \n4. Indian Film Festival In \nRishikesh  \nMarch 2018 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |699", "t": "Kisan TV", "head": "   \n  GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n MINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 699 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019) \n \nKISAN TV \n699.  SHRI K. PARASURAMAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state:  \n(a)  the details about the number of telecasting centres established by the \nGovernment for Kisan TV in Tamil Nadu;  \n(b)  whether the number of research related programmes in agriculture, telecasted \nin Kisan TV is less and even Kisan TV is not available to people w ho avail \nprivate DTH companies; and  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government to telecast \nKisan TV channel to all parts of the country particularly in rural areas?  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nINFORMATION AND BROADCASTING  \n{COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)} \n  \n(a)  Prasar Bharati has informed that DD Kisan channel is uplinked from New \nDelhi, which is available on Doordarshan’s Free -to-Air DTH service, the signals of \nwhich can be received anywhere in the country (except Andaman & Nicobar Islands) \nincluding Tamil Nadu, with the help of small sized dish receive unit.  \n(b)  The programmes for telecast on DD Kisan are  backed by research, provided \nby scientists from the Knowledge Partners such as  Indian Council of Agricultural \nResearch, Indian Agriculture Research Institute, Indian Metrological Department and \nNational Dairy Development Board. \nAll private DTH operators, MSO (Multiple System O perators) and Cable \nNetwork are mandated to carry all channels of Doordarshan including DD Kisan \nunder the “Must Carry Channel” orders given by the Ministry of Information & \nBroadcasting. \n(c)  DD Kisan Channel is available in the bouquet of Doordarshan’s DTH Service, \nthe signal of which can be received anywhere in the country (except Andaman & \nNicobar Islands), including rural areas, with the help of a small sized dish receive \nunit. \nIn addition to above, digital High Power Transmitters of Doordarshan, one \neach at Delhi, Raipur & Ranchi, are also transmitting Kisan TV channel “DD Kisan” \nin terrestrial mode. \n*****\n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |7", "t": "Investment by Indian/Foreign Companies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.†*7 \nANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nNEW TOURISM PROJECTS \n \n†*7. SHRI LALLU SINGH:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has recently proposed to launc h new \ntourism projects to promote tourism at large scale in the \ncountry;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether the Government has sanctioned such projects for \nWest Bengal which has immense potential for tourism \ndevelopment; and  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI K.J. ALPHONS) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n******** \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.†*7 ANSWERED ON  04.02.2019 \nREGARDING NEW TOURISM PROJECTS. \n \n(a) and (b):  Ministry of Tourism has taken several initiatives for \ndevelopment and promotion of tourism at large scale in the country \nwhich includes the following: \n \ni. Launched the Swadesh Darshan Scheme with a  vision to \ndevelop theme based tourist circuits. \nii. Launched the National Mission on Pilgrimage \nRejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive \n(PRASHAD) Scheme for holistic development of identified \npilgrimage destinations. \niii. Identified 17 Ico nic Tourist sites in the country for \ndevelopment. \niv. Launched the Adopt a Heritage Project for development \nand maintenance of tourist amenities at heritage \nsites/monuments and other tourist sites. \nv. Development and promotion of ‘Niche Tourism’ products \nto attract tourist with specific interest and to ensure \nrepeat visits for the unique products in which India has a \ncomparative advantage. \nvi. Launched 24x7 toll free Multi-Lingual Tourist Helpline. \nvii. Promoting India as a holistic tourism destination \nincluding its various tourism sites and products through \nIncredible India 2.0 Campaign in important and potential \ntourism generating markets. \nviii. Providing facility of e -Visa for 5 sub -categories i.e. e -\nTourist visa, e -Business visa, e -Medical visa, e -Medical \nAttendant visa and e-Conference visa for nationals of 166 \ncountries. \n \n(c) and (d):  Ministry of Tourism has sanctioned projects having \npotential for tourism development in West Bengal under its various \nschemes which is given at Annexure. \n********\n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (c) AND (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.†*7 ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 REGARDING NEW TOURISM \nPROJECTS. \n \nSwadesh Darshan Scheme            (Rs. in Crore) \nSl. \nNo. \nState Name \nof the \nCircuit \nName of the Project Amt. \nSanctioned \nAmt. \nReleased \nYear 2015-16 \n1. West \nBengal \nCoastal \nCircuit \nDevelopment of Beach \nCircuit: Udaipur - Digha- \nShankarpur- Tajpur- \nMandarmani- Fraserganj-\nBakkhlai- Henry Island in \nWest Bengal \n85.39 61.96 \n \nPRASHAD Scheme            (Rs. in Crore) \nSl. \nNo. \nState Name of the Project Project Cost Amt. \nReleased \nYear 2016-17 \n1. West Bengal Development of Belur 30.03 23.39 \n \nScheme for Assistance to Central Agencies for Tourism Infrastructure \nDevelopment  \n(Rs. in Crore) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of Project Division(s) Amount \nSanctioned \nAmount \nReleased \nDate of \nRelease \n1. Joint Development of \nTourism Amenities at \nRampurhat Railway \nStation \nHowrah \nDivision \n3.48 1.74 29.03.2018 \n2. Joint Development of \nTourism Amenities at \nTarakeshwar Railway \nStation \nHowrah \nDivision \n3.86 1.93 29.03.2018 \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |70", "t": "Textile Exports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO-70 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON-07.02.2019 \nINTERNATIONAL SOLAR ALLIANCE \n \n*70. SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state:- \n(a) whether the Government has recently approved to move a resolution in the \nFirst Assembly of the International Solar Alliance (ISA) to open up its \nmembership to all UN countries; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the aim of the resolution; and \n \n(c) the manner in which it will help in putting solar energy in global agenda \nwith universal appeal for developing and deploying solar energy? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND \nPOWER (I/C) (SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a)  to (c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement \n \nStatement referred to in Reply to parts (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha  \nStarred Question No. 70 to be answered on 7 February 2019 \nregarding   \n \nInternational Solar Alliance \n \nThe requisite information in reply to the Question, is as under:  \n \n \nThe Indian delegation to the International Solar Alliance (ISA) moved a Resolution in the \nfirst Assembly of the ISA held on 3 October 2018, to amend the Framework Agreement of \nISA for opening up the ISA membership to all countries that are members of United Nations. \nThe Assembly considered and adopted the Resolution. The Cabinet, in its meeting held on 1 \nNovember 2018, accorded ex-post facto approval for moving the Resolution.  \n \n \nIt is recognized that solar energy can play a  major role in achieving energy security ; \nSustainable Development Goals , improving the conditions and standard of living for the \nmajority of the world’s population.  Opening up ISA membership will help in  putting solar \nenergy on the global agenda for ensur ing access to sustainable and modern energy for all at \naffordable rates. This will ensure that the ISA initiative benefit s the world at large and is not \nrestricted to the countries lying either fully or partially between the Tropics of Cancer and \nCapricorn.  \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |700", "t": "Allocation of Funds to Sports Federations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 700 \nTO BE ANSWERD ON 07.02.2019 \n \nAllocation of Funds to Sports Federations \n \n700.  DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \nSHRI ASHOK SHANKARRAO CHAVAN: \n \nWill the  Minister of YOUTH  AFFAIRS AND SPORTS  be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of funds allocated by  the Government to the \nrecognized sports  federations in the country since 2014, year  and \nfederation-wise; \n(b) the percentage of utilization of  these funds by t he sports \nfederations during the said period, year and federation-wise; \n(c) whether any instances of misuse  of funds by NSFs have come to \nthe notice of the Government during the said period  and if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the  action taken thereon  by the \nGovernment; \n(d) whether the Government  undertakes an audit of these sports  \nfederations on an annual basis and if so,  whether any cases of \nfinancial mismanagement have been discovered; \n(e) if so, the details thereof along with  the steps taken by the  \nGovernment to rectify such instances of mismanagement; and \n(f) whether NSFs have not paid  adequate attention to the promotion \nof women players in various sports including  cricket and if so, the \nreasons therefor alongwith the reaction of the Government thereto? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[COL. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE(RETD.)] \n \n(a) to (e) Information is being compiled and will be laid on the \nTable of the House. \n\n \n(f) National Sports Federations (NSFs) are responsible for \npromotion and development of respective sports disciplines both for \nmen and women players. No substantiated instance of NSFs not \npaying adequate attention to promotion of women players has been \nestablished.  \n \n*****  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |701", "t": "Power Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.701 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nPOWER PRODUCTION \n \n†701.   SHRI RAHUL KASWAN: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has made  progress in area of power production in \nthe country; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof, State -wise including Rajasthan during the last \nthree years; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has  formulated any special scheme to increase  \nmore power production; and \n \n(d)  if so, the areas and States in which  action plan of power prod uction has \nbeen decided? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER  \nAND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n( SHRI  R. K. SINGH ) \n \n(a) & (b):  Yes, Madam. The details of   power generation from conventional \nfuel sources (Thermal, Hydro & Nuclear) from 25 MW & above capacity and \nrenewable sources in the country, State -wise, including Rajasthan during the last \nthree years & the current year 2018-19 (up to December 2018) are given at Annex-\nI & Annex-II respectively. \n \n(c) & (d): As on 31.12.20 18, the installed generation capacity is about 349 \nGiga Watt (GW) which is more than sufficient to meet the peak power demand of \nthe country of around 177 GW which occurred during the current year 2018 -19 \n(upto Decembe r, 2018). T aking into account the futu re demand projections, \ngeneration capacity addition of 47,855 MW coal, 406 MW Gas, 6,823 MW Hydro \nand 3,300 MW Nuclear is projected to be commissioned during 2017 -2022.  The \nrenewable energy capacity is also targeted to be 175 GW by 2021 -22 on All India \nbasis.  \nWith these generation capacity additions, the electricity peak \ndemand of 225 GW and energy requirement of 1566 BU projected for the year \n2021-22 as per the 19th Electric Power Survey, will be fully met on All India basis. \n************** \n\n \n \nANNEX-I \n \nANNEX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.  701 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON \n07.02.2019. \n*************** \n \nState-wise Generation for conventional sources for Last 3 years and current year  \n(upto December, 2018) \n \nState  \nGeneration (in MUs) \n2018-19 (upto-Dec. 18)    2017-18 2016-17     2015-16 \nANDAMAN & NICOBAR 99.98 258.79 215.56 182.85 \nANDHRA PRADESH 47281.46 61851.80 65248.16 58230.59 \nARUNACHAL PRADESH 1339.98 1416.74 1249.01 1280.25 \nASSAM 5381.26 5972.12 5981.37 4