\n 3 Arunachal Pradesh 1.28 0.70 27.43 18.44 \n 4 Assam 16.57 24.49 14.15 90.94 \n 5 Bihar 246.42 309.06 292.53 165.11 \n 6 Chandigarh 11.56 7.87 13.16 3.40 \n 7 Chhattisgarh 744.38 1069.21 1446.22 1202.42 \n 8 Dadra and Nagar Haveli 3.89 5.23 1.31 0.50 \n 9 Daman & Diu 13.09 18.53 14.43 4.25 \n 10 Delhi 221.25 241.09 144.73 128.97 \n 11 DVC 1.81 9.54 14.09 118.26 \n 12 Gujarat 10666.47 11759.63 9497.99 8003.73 \n 13 Haryana 479.62 560.70 449.54 1343.15 \n 14 HP 2074.25 1903.05 2015.58 1921.77 \n 15 J & K 260.89 324.37 326.12 304.79 \n 16 Jharkhand 13.92 19.47 38.47 19.77 \n 17 Karnataka 16479.89 13463.98 9585.68 10061.03 \n 18 Kerala 618.85 77"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |702", "t": "Rubberized-Bitumen for Construction", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 702 \nANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2019 \n  \nRUBBERIZED-BITUMEN FOR CONSTRUCTION \n \n702.  DR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT: \nSHRI DHANANJAY MAHADIK: \nSHRI MOHITE PATIL VIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO: \nSHRI SATAV RAJEEV: \nDR. J. JAYAVARDHAN: \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total length of rubberized roads  constructed in the country during the last three years \nand  the current year, State-wise along with the expenditure incurred thereon; \n(b) whether the Government has  conducted any study regarding the  difference between  \nbituminous and rubberized roads and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government/National  Highways Authority of India (NHAI) are  \ncontemplating to use rubberized bitumen  in the construction of National Highway  roads \nin the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether most of the roads under  NHAI are in bad condition leading to fatal  accidents; \nand \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the  action taken/being taken by the Government  to construct \nquality roads in the country? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n(a)  The State-wise rubberized roads which have been constructed during the last three year \nand as on 31st Dec 2018 in current financial year are given in Annexure-I. And the expenditure is \nnot incurred separately so the overall expenditure incurred during the last three year and current \nyear is given in Annexure-II.  \n \n(b) to (c)  No such study has been done during the last three years . Ministry & National \nHighway Authority of India  are contemplating to use rubberized bitumen as per the IRC SP -53-\n2010 i.e. Guidelines on use of Modified Bitumen in Road Construction.  \n \n \n\n \n(d) to (e)  Development and maintenance of National Highways is a continuous process and \nthey are kept in traffic worthy condition . Works of National Highway are executed duly \nfollowing the specifications and standards laid down by Ministry of Road Transport & Highways \n(MoRT&H) and I ndian Road Congress (IRC). To ensure that the works are executed as per \nprescribed specifications and standards, Consultants are appointed by Ministry and its agencies \nfor day to day su pervision of the works at site. Officials of Ministry/National Highways \nAuthority of India/ National Highways & Infrastructure Development Corporation Limited  also \ncarries out quality checks of the works executed on random  basis and deficiencies, if any, \nobserved during such examination / inspection are brought to the notice of the Concessionaire  / \nContractor for corrective measures. The Authority Engineer/ Independent Engineer along with \nthe road safety experts are also engaged for Audit & Quality check of roads to ensure road safety \n& its construction  quality. In case of any defaults, actions aga inst the defaulting agencies are \ntaken as per the provisions of agreement. \n  \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN PART (a) OF REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 702 FOR ANSWER ON 07.02.2019 ASKED BY DR. HEENA \nVIJAYKUMAR GAVIT , SHRI DHANANJAY MAHADIK, SHRI MO HITE PATIL \nVIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO, SHRI SATAV RAJEEV, DR. J. JAYAVARDHAN AND \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE  REGARDING RUBBERIZED-BITUMEN FOR \nCONSTRUCTION. \n \nThe State-wise rubberized roads which have been constructed during the last three year and as on \n31st Dec in current financial year \n \n \nS No State/UTs Rubberized Roads Constructed Length (km) \n2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 \n1 Assam 15.55 0.00 0.00 0.00 \n2 Kerala 23.10 7.52 25.1 77.8 \n3 Maharashtra/Goa 41.40 39.06 46.23 18.87 \n4 Tamil Nadu 66.27 218.94 92.74 258.89 \n5 Telangana/Puducherry 233.89 74.33 33.29 47.00 \n  Total 380.21 339.85 197.36 402.56 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE-II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN PART (a) OF REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 702 FOR ANSWER ON 07.02.2019 ASKED BY DR. HEENA \nVIJAYKUMAR GAVIT , SHRI DHANANJAY MAHADIK, SHRI MO HITE PATIL \nVIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO, SHRI SATAV RAJEEV, DR. J. JAYAVARDHAN AND \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE  REGARDING RUBBERIZED-BITUMEN FOR \nCONSTRUCTION. \nThe State-wise expenditure incurred in the development of National Highways during the last \nthree year and current financial year \n \nSl. \nNo. \nStates/UTs/Schemes/Agency \n(Sl. 1 to 33 contains allocation \nunder NH(O), NHDP-IV through \nState PWD, EAP, PBFF) \nExpenditure (Rs Crore) \n2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 up \nto Dec \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1,000.43 2,014.50 1,780.70 1,435.95 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 1.83 90.66 62.05 65.73 \n3 Assam 78.09 137.80 307.75 2.42 \n4 Bihar 707.86 1,331.61 1,523.88 1,106.79 \n5 Chhattisgarh 339.80 1,495.68 1,071.58 940.84 \n6 Goa 119.98 434.51 527.48 709.74 \n7 Gujarat 225.77 213.41 163.29 241.03 \n8 Haryana 89.85 161.27 105.17 273.65 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 13"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |703", "t": "Investigation by CBI", "head": " \nLOK SABHA \n UN-STARRED QUESTION NO. 703 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nIVESTIGATION BY CBI \n \n703: SHRI LAXMI NARAYAN YADAV \n        SHRIMATI RAMA DEVI \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंरी   \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) is investigating the \ncorruption case worth crores in National Handloom Development Corporation \nLimited(NHDC); \n(b)    if so, the details thereof; \n(c)   whether top officials of NHDC and weaver organization are indirectl y \nopposing CBI investigation through various mediums including protest etc; and \n(d)    the response of the Government in this regard?  \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्य मंरी  (श्री अजय टम्टा) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES \n(SHRI AJAY TAMTA) \n \n \n(a) to (d) NHDC has referred a matter to Central Bureau of Investigation \n(CBI) for investigation in to the matter of irregularities in the implementation of \nyarn supply scheme in NHDC Lucknow Branch office. \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |704", "t": "Facilities to Para-Athletes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n  MINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 704 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nFacilities to Para-Athletes \n \n704.  SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL:  \n   \n         Will the Minister of YOUTH  AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of steps taken/being taken by the Government to provide world \nclass facilities to para -athletes to train them for various International \ncompetitions including Asian Games , Commonwealth Games and Olympic \nGames;  \n(b) whether the Government has taken any steps to set up facilities for \nrehabilitation, fitness training and sports science for para -athletes and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n(c) whether the para -athletes are given assistance under Target Olympic \nPodium Scheme (TOPS) or any other scheme and if so, the details thereof; \nand  \n(d) the details of measures taken by the Government to make the existing \nstadiums, training centres and other sport facilities  more accessible to the \npara-athletes?   \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR YOUTH AFFAIRS \nAND SPORTS \n(COL. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (RETD.)) \n \n(a) to (c)  Preparation of Indian sportspersons / teams for international \ncompetitions including Asian Games, Commonwealth Games and Olympic \nGames is an ongoing process. This Ministry has recognised Paralympic \nCommittee of India (PCI) as National Sports Federation ( NSF) for promotion \nof sports among para -athletes and grants maximum financial assistance. \nUnder the Scheme of Assistance to National Sports Federations, financial \nassistance is provided to NSFs, including the PCI, for preparation of athletes. \n\nThe funding cove rs, inter -alia, expenditure on equipment, state -of-the-art \ninfrastructure, lodging, services of reputed Indian and Foreign \nCoaches/supporting staff, scientific & medical support, training abroad and \nparticipation in international competitions in India and abroad. Further, Para-\nathletes are also given assistance under the Target Olympic Podium (TOP) \nScheme for their customized training within the country and abroad.  In \naddition, athletes included in TOPS are given out of pocket allowance at the \nrate of Rs. 50,000 per month. The Khelo India Scheme being implemented by \nthis Ministry also has a vertical, namely, Promotion of Sports among persons \nwith disabilities” for targeted support to sportspersons with disabilities.   \n \n Sports Science/ Medicine Centres have  been established in the \nRegional Centres and Academic Institutions under Sports Authority of India \n(SAI). List of such centres is in Annexure – I. Further, the Government \nprovides financial assistance to different institutions for establishment of \nsports science/medicine department. The list of institutions, who have been \nselected for financial assistance for the purpose is in Annex ure – II. These \ncentres will cater to the needs of all athletes, including para athletes.  \n \n(d) Sports is a State subject and development of sports, including \ndeveloping infrastructural support to sportspersons, is primarily the \nresponsibility of the State  Governments. Accordingly, making existing \nstadiums, training centres and other sport facilities more accessible to the \npara-athletes is the responsibility of owners of the facilities under different \nstatutes of the Government. As regards facilities of the  different institutions \nunder the aegis of this Ministry is concerned, they are being made  disabled \nfriendly.  \n \nThe Government has, however, identified the Netaji Subhas Western \nCentre, Gandhinagar of Sports Authority of India (SAI) and developed it as the \nmain centre for holding sports activities pertaining to differently -abled \nathletes.  \n  \n***** \n\nANNEXURE- I \n \nANNEXURE RE FERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION 704.  FOR ANSWER ON 07.02.2019 ASKED BY SHRI \nRAJENDRA AGRAWAL REGARDING ‘FACILITIES TO PARA-ATHLETES’ \n \n1. SAI Netaji Subhas National Institute of Sports, Patiala \n2. SAI, Netaji Subhash Engineering College, Kolkata \n3. SAI Netaji Subhash Southern Centre, Bengaluru \n4. SAI Headquarters, New Delhi \n5. SAI Netaji Subhash Western Centre, Gandhi Nagar \n6. Lakshmibai National College of Physical Education, Thiruvananthapuram.  \n7. SAI Central Center, Bhopal \n8. SAI Regional Centre, Lucknow \n9. SAI, Northern Center, Sonepat \n10. SAI, Netaji Subhas North Eastern Centre, Imphal \n11. SAI, Netaji Subhas North-East Regional Centre, Guwahati \n  \n\nANNEXURE - II \n \nANNEXURE RE FERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c ) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION 704.  FOR ANSWER ON 07.02.2019 ASKED BY SHRI \nRAJENDRA AGRAWAL REGARDING ‘FACILITIES TO PARA-ATHLETES’ \n \n \nList of Universities/Institutions selected for funding to support  sports science/medicine \ndepartment \n \n1. Guru Nanak Dev University, Amritsar \n2. Lakshmibai National Institute of Physical Education, Gwalior \n3. National Institute of Nutrition, Hyderabad \n4. Annamalai University, Tamil"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |706", "t": "Setting up of New TV and Radio Channels", "head": "        \n  GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n MINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 706 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019) \n \nSETTING UP OF NEW TV AND RADIO CHANNELS \n706.  SHRI PRABHAKAR REDDY KOTHA: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state:  \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to setup new TV and Radio channels in \nthe State of Telangana and Andhra Pradesh and if so, the details thereof and \nif not, the reasons therefor;  \n(b)  the details of how such TV and Radio channels will be managed and \noperated;  \n(c)  whether the Government also proposes to extend the coverage of \nDoordarshan and All India Radio in the newly formed districts of Telangana \nState; and  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nINFORMATION AND BROADCASTING  \n{COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)} \n  \n(a) & (b) Prasar Bharati has informed that they have  an approved scheme of \nsetting up of AIR FM transmitters with studio at Nellore and relay station at 8 \nplaces in the State of Telangana and Andhra Pradesh. Details are given in \nAnnexure-1 & 2. \nDoordarshan \n \nDoordarshan is already operating DD Channels from Telangana and Andhra \nPradesh states as per details below:- \n \n(i) DD Yadagiri (Telangana) \n(ii) DD Saptagiri (Andhra Pradesh)  \n  \n\n \n(c) & (d)  All India Radio: \nThe S tate of Telangana already has 15 transmitters (MW -2, SW -1 & FM -12) \ninstalled at 11 places having  coverage of about 99% of the area and 99% of the \ntotal population. Details are given in Annexure-3. \n \nDoordarshan: \n \nAll the areas of the country (including Telangana State), have been provided with \nmulti-channel TV coverage through Doordarshan’s free -to-air DTH service. DTH \nsignals can be received anywhere in the country (including all the districts of \nTelangana State) with the help of small sized dish receive units. All the 24X7 \nDoordarshan channels including “DD Yadagiri” channel originating fr om \nTelangana are available on DTH Platform. \n \n*****\n\nAnnexure-1 \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PAR T (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 706 FOR ANSWER ON 07.02.2019 \n \nApproved AIR Station with limited studio facility in the state of Telangana and Andhra \nPradesh \n \nSr. \nNos. \nPLACE STATE POWER  OF FM \nTRANSMITTER \n1.  NELLORE ANDHRA PRADESH 10 KW \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure-2 \n \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PAR T (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 706 FOR ANSWER ON 07.02.2019 \n \nApproved Relay Stations in the state of Telangana and Andhra Pradesh \n \nSr. \nNos. \nPLACE STATE POWER  OF FM \nTRANSMITTER \n1.  KAKINADA ANDHRA PRADESH 10 KW \n2.  ALAGADDA ANDHRA PRADESH 100 W \n3.  MADANAPALLE ANDHRA PRADESH 100 W \n4.  RAJAHMUNDRY (IN PLACE \nOF BHEEMAVARAM) \nANDHRA PRADESH 100 W \n5.  DAVARKONDA TELANGANA 100 W \n6.  NALGONDA TELANGANA 100 W \n7.  NIRMAL TELANGANA 100 W \n8.  RAMAGUNDAM TELANGANA 100 W \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-3 \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PAR T (c) & (d) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 706 FOR ANSWER ON 07.02.2019 \n \nExisting AIR setup in the state of Telangana \nExisting Radio Stations- 11 Nos.  :    Transmitters :15 (MW-2, FM-12, SW-1) \n \n***** \n \n \nS. No. \n \nStations Type/ Power of the existing  Transmitter Studio facilities \n AM \n(MW/SW) \nFM \n1.  Adilabad  10 kW FM  Multipurpose \nStudio \n2.  Hyderabad 200 kW MW \n20 KW MW \n50 KW SW \n 10 KW   \n 10 kW  \n Type-IV, Uplink, \nRegional News \nUnit, \nStereo , News-\non-Phone \n3.  Karimnagar ----- 5  kW   \n4.  Kothagudam ----- 6 KW Multipurpose \nStudio \n5.  Nizamabad  ----- 10 KW  \n6.  Suryapet - 1 kW (interim set up)  \n7. Warangal ----- 10 kW Multipurpose \nStudio \n8. Kammareddy ----- 100 W FM  (Relay)  \n9. Banswara ----- 100 W FM (Relay)  \n10.  Mehboobnagar -- 10 kW FM  (Relay)  \n11. Khammam --- 100 W FM  (Relay)  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |707", "t": "Tariff Payable to Service Providers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 707 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019) \n \nTARIFF PAYABLE TO SERVICE PROVIDERS \n \n707. DR. C. GOPALAKRISHNAN : \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has recently notified the changes / revision of Tariff \npayable to the service providers to view each Television Channel (Pay Channel) \nas per the choice of subscribers; \n \n(b) If so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) The time by which new tariff will come into force; \n \n(d) whether the Government has appointed any supervisory body for proper \nimplementation of the new tariff; and  \n \n(e) If so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nINFORMATION AND BROADCASTING {COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)}  \n(a) to  (c)  In order to ensure orderly growth of the sector, Telecom Regulatory \nAuthority of India (TRAI) issued following regulations / Tariff Order afte r due \nconsultation process : \ni. The Telecommunication (Broadcasting and Cable) Services (Eighth) \n(Addressable Systems) Tariff Order, 2017 dated 3rd March 2017, as \namended on 30th March 2017 [the Tariff Order 2017]; \n \nii. The Telecommunication (Broadcasting and Cable) Services \nInterconnection (Addressable Systems) Regulations, 2017 dated 3rd \nMarch 2017 [the Interconnection Regulation 2017]; \n \niii. The Telecommunication (Broadcasting and Cable) Services Standards \nof Quality of Service and Consumer Protection (Addressabl e Systems) \nRegulations, 2017 dated 3rd March 2017 [the QoS Regulation 2017]. \n \nHowever, the said regulatory framework could not be implemented due to some \npending litigations. The framework was duly notified vide press release no. 71/2018 \ndated 3rd July 2018 with revised timelines.   \n \nAs per the implementation schedule, all the service providers were required to \ncomply with the provisions of the tariff order by 29 th December 2018.  However, \nkeeping in view the consumer convenience and to provide suf ficient time to the \n\nconsumers for exercising their options, the Authority extended the last date of \nexercising the choice by consumers until 31st January 2019 vide its press release \nno. 127/2018 dated 28/12/2018.   \n \n(d) & (e) TRAI  is monitoring the implementat ion of the New Regulatory \nFramework as per the provisions of the TRAI Act, 1997. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |708", "t": "National Highways Accidents Relief Service Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 708 \n[[ \nANSWERED  ON  7th  FEBRUARY, 2019 \nNATIONAL  HIGHWAYS  ACCIDENTS  RELIEF  SERVICE  SCHEME \n†708.   SHRI  LAKHAN  LAL  SAHU:  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Union Government has received requests from the States/UTs to \nprovide cranes and ambul ances under „National Highways Accidents Relief Service \nScheme‟ during the last three years and current year;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof alongwith the number of cranes and ambulances provided \nto the States/UTs during the said period;  \n(c) whether the Union Government propose to launch a Trauma Attendant  Mobile \nVehicle/Two Wheeler Vehicle Service with the name of „First Respond‟ to provide an \nemergency medical care to the victims of road accidents in the entire country; and   \n(d) if so, the details ther eof and the time by which these facilities are likely to be \nprovided? \n ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI  MANSUKH  L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a)  & (b) :  The National Highways Accidents Relief Service Scheme was implemented by the \nMinistry of Road Transport & Highways during the 12 th Five Year Plan period. Under the \nscheme the Ministry provided f inancial assistance to States/UT s for procurement of  \nambulances/ cranes. During the FY  2015-16, Ministry has provided financial assistance to the \nfollowing State government for procurement of Basic Life Supports Ambulances (BLSA):- \n \nSl. No. State No. of BLSA \n1 Jammu and Kashmir 136 \n2  Sikkim 31 \n3 Mizoram 05 \n \n \n \n \n\n \n(c ) & (d)  The Government has launched a mobile app for highway users i.e. “SukhadYatra \n1033” which enables highways users to report safety hazards on National Highways including \naccidents. GPS enabled ambulances connected with a central control room through a toll -free \nnumber 1033, ar e stationed to transport the injured to the hospitals for treatment within the \n„Golden Hour‟. As part of incident management under operation and maintenance of the NH \nstretches, Highway Ambulances are deployed in their respective service area to extend \nassistance in case of any emergency on National Highways.  \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |709", "t": "Navigable Waterways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 709\nTo BE ANSWERED oN 7\" FEBUARY, 2019\nNAVIGABLE WATERWAYS\n709 SHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN:\nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state'\ndrd qftd-{r d4\n(a) whether certain inland waterways projects have been sanctioned Lry the Government in\nMaharashtra;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the number of navigable waterways identified by the\nGovernment for commercial transportation in the State and the nurnber of MoUs signed\nwith ports trusts and comPanies;\n(c) the funds sanctioned by the Government for the said projects; and\n(d) the details of cargo handled through inland waterways during e,ach of the last three\nyears, State-wise including Maharashtra?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPITIG\n(sHRl PON. RADHAKRISHNAN)\n(a) to (c) To promote lnland Water Transport in the country, 111 (including 5 existing and\niOo new; National Waterways (NWs) spread over24 states have been declared under the\nNational Waterways Act, 2016.15 NWs are in the State of Maharashtra ils at Annex-1.\nDirections have been issued by the Ministry of Shipping on 18.0'4.2018 under Section\n111 of the Major port Trusts Act, 1963 to five major port trusts to undertake development of\nNational Waterways allocated to each of them through release of grants by lnland Waterways\nAuthority of lndia (lwAl). Accordingly, development of 11 NWs in tlre Maharashtra have\nbeen allocated to Jawaharlal Nehru Port Trust (JNPT). Details of these NWs are at Annex-2.\nThe development of National Wateruuays is undertaken through Gross Budgetary\nSupport (GBS), Extra Budgetary Resources (EBRs) and multilateral assistance provided by\nthe Government to lWAl. fhe details of funds provided since 2015-16 for implementation of\ndevelopmental works on NWs and preparation of feasibility studies and detailed project\nreports, including the NWs in Maharashtra are tabulated below:\n(Rs. in crore)\nYear BE RE\n2015-16 300.00 (Grants) 298 72 (Grants)\n2016- 17 BE 350.00 (Grants) + 340(EBRs)- 303.00 (Grants) + EBRs 340.00\n2017 - 18 228.00 (Grants) + 660.00 (EBRs)- 335.32 (Grants)+ EBRs 660'oo\n2018 -1 9 399.75(Grants) 780.29 (Grants)\n(*) Extra BudgetarY Resources\nThe details of cargo transported through operational NWs in the last three years is at\nAnnex-3.\n(d)\n\n-2-\nAnnex.l\nList of National waterwavs in the state of Maharashtra\nNational WaterwaY\nNational WaterwaYs-4\nKrishna - Godavari including Kakinada and Eluru canal and North and South\nBuckinqham canals\nAmba (NW-10)\nOaOfrot Creef- Vashishti River (NW-28)\nffindalikaRiver (NW-85)\nSairrtri (Bankot Creek) (NW-89)\nKdyan -ffirway - Vasai Creek -Ulhas River (NW-53)\nNarmada (NW-73)\nTapi (NW-100)\nSfiashtr'r Rrver-Jaigad Creek (NW-g1 )\nRajpuri Creek (NW-83)\nRrunawati-Aran (NW-1 1 )\nWalnganga- Pranahita (NW-1 09)\nNag (NW-72)\nManiara (NW-70)\nPanganga-Ward ha (NW-78)\n\n-3-\nAnnex-2\nList of National Waterwavs allocated for development to Jawaharlal Nehru Port Trust\n(JNPT)\nSl. No. NWs Number Name of Waterways Distance\n(Km.)\nRemarks\n1 NW-10 Amba River 45 DF'R under\nPreparation\n2 NW-11 Arunavati - Aran River 98 Feasibility Reports\nbe nq finalized\n3. NW-28 Dabhol Creek - Vashisti\nRiver\n45 DF'R under\nPrr:paration\n4 NW-53 Kalyan-Thane-Mumbai\nWaterway Vasai Creek\nUlhas River\n145 DF'R under\nconsideration. Being\nprerpared by Thane\nMunicipal Corporation\n5. NW-70 Manjara River 242 Feasibility Reports\nbe nq finalized\n6 NW-72 Nag River 60 Feasibility Reports\nbe nq finalized\n7. NW_78 Penganga-Wardha River 265 Feasibility Reports\nbernq finalized\n8. NW-85 Revdanda Creek -\nKundalika River\n31 DF'R under\nPreparation\n9. NW_89 Bankot Creek - Savitri\nRiver\n46 DF'R under\nPreparation\n10. NW-100 Tapi River 436 DF'R under\nPreparation\n11. NW-109 Wainganga-Pranahita\nRiver\n164 Feasibility Reports\nbe ng finalized\n\n-+-\nCarqo transported on National Wate\nAnnex-3\n(in Million Tonnes)\nYEAR NW.1 NW-2 NW-3 NWs\nin\nGoa#\nNWs in\nMaharashtr\na#\nNWs in\nGujarat\n#\nTotal\n2014-15 s.05 0.52 097 654\n2015-16 624 060 1.06 7.90\n2016-17 4.62 0.61 103 15 65 33.29 NA 55 20\n2017 -18 548 0.56 042 1 1.09 25.96 11.52 55.03\n# lnland Waterways in Goa, Maharashtra and Gujarat declared as \"National Watenruays\"\non 12th April, 2016."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |71", "t": "National Skill Qualification Framework (NSQF)", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 71 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2019 \n \nNATIONAL SKILL QUALIFICATION FRAMEWORK (NSQF) \n \n71. SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB: \n  SHRI SANJAY DHOTRE:  \nSHRI RAHUL SHEWALE: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a)  the salient features of the National Skill Qua lification Framework (NSQF) and other \nmajor schemes/programmes of the Government for impa rting vocational education/training \nfor development of skill of youth in the country;  \n(b)  whether the Government has provided adequate f unds under the said \nschemes/programmes during each of the last three years and the current year;  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof, Scheme/ Programme- wise and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(d)  the number of complaints of irregularities/cor ruption/diversion of funds in \nimplementation of the said schemes/ programmes rece ived by the Government during the \nsaid period, Scheme/ `Programmewise along with the action taken/being taken thereon;  \n(e)  whether the Government has accepted the recomm endations of the National \nKnowledge Commission pertaining to increase of vocational education in the country; \n (f)  if so, the details thereof along with the imp lementation status of such accepted \nrecommendations; and \n (g)  the other steps taken/being taken by the Gove rnment in this direction? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI ANANTKUMAR HEGDE) \n(a) to (c)  The National Skills Qualification Frame work (NSQF) organizes qualifications \naccording to a series of levels of knowledge, skill s and aptitude. These levels are defined in \nterms of learning outcomes which the learner must possess regardless of whether they were \nacquired through formal, non-formal or informal learning. NSQF is a quality assurance and a \nnationally integrated education and competency base d skill framework that provides for \nhorizontal as well as vertical mobility between voc ational education, skill training, general \neducation and technical education. This enables a p erson to acquire desired competency \nlevels, transit to the job market and, at an opport une time, return for acquiring additional \nskills to further upgrade their competences.  \n \nThe Government is implementing a number of schemes for imparting employable \nskills to the youth through short term and long ter m training. Ministry of Skill Development \nand Entrepreneurship (MSDE) is implementing its fla gship scheme Pradhan Mantri Kaushal \nVikas Yojana (PMKVY) 2016-20 on pan –India basis un der Skill India Mission with an \nobjective to provide skilling to one crore people u nder Short Term Training (STT), \nRecognition of Prior Learning (RPL) and Special Pro ject (SP) across the country for four \nyears i.e. 2016-2020 with an outlay of Rs 12,000 cr ore  through accredited and affiliated \ntraining partner/training centers. \n \n\nThe Financial Year-wise funds allocated under PMKVY are as under: \n \nFY  Funds disbursed / sanctioned to implementing agency (in Rs cr.)  \n2016-17 699.99 \n2017-18 1721.18 \n2018-19 1476.39 \nTotal 3897.56 \n \nDirectorate General of Training (DGT), MSDE imparts  long term trainings through \nIndustrial Training Institutes (ITIs) and is also e ntrusted with the responsibility of vocational \ntraining in the country. One of the Flagship scheme s is “Craftsmen Training Scheme” being \nimplemented through a network of 15,042  ITIs locat ed all over the country with an objective \nto provide skilled work force to the industry in 13 8 trades with duration of 6 months, 1 year \nand 2 years. All these 138 trades are NSQF aligned. \n (d)  NSDC has put in place a specific grievance address al mechanism and a dedicated \nteam takes care of the grievances received through online system, grievance portal and via \nRTIs. Further, under the scheme, PMKVY 2016-20 Moni toring Committee review and \nformulate framework on the critical cases related t o monitoring issues of the stakeholders. \nAlso, the Committee has formulated penalty grid (ap proved by Steering Committee for \nPMKVY) for taking action against erring / non compl iant training centres / stakeholders. \nBased on the penalty grid, National Skill Developme nt Corporation (NSDC) has suspended \ntraining centers for noncompliance of PMKVY 2016-20  guidelines on multiple parameters \nand periodically publishes the list on PMKVY official website. \n \ne) to g)  The National Knowledge Commission gave va rious recommendations \npertaining to increase of vocational education in t he country in 2006. The Government has \ntaken various initiatives in this direction. The MSDE has issued a Gazette Notification for the \nformation of “National Council for Vocational Educa tion and Training (NCVET)” on 5 \nth  \nDecember, 2018. The NCVET is envisaged to look into  development, qualitative \nimprovement and regulation of vocational education and training, "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |711", "t": "Utilisation of Funds by Panchayats", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.711 \nANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nUtilisation of Funds by Panchayats \n \n†711. SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \n  \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether  the Government proposes to make any provisions with regard to action to be taken \nagainst such Gram Panchayats which are not utilising the funds provided to them under the 14 th \nFinance Commission; \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether any time limit is prescribed to utilise the said funds and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d) the details of action to be taken against the Gram Panchayat failing to utilise the said funds \nwithin the prescribed time frame? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) & (b) There is no such proposal. However, d etailed guidelines have been issued by Ministry of \nFinance (MoF) dated 8.10.2015 for implementation of the Fourteenth Finance Commission \n(FFC)grant. The guidelines provide that expenditures are to be incurred by Gram Panchayats (GPs) \nfor delivering basic services within the functions devolved to them under the State laws after proper \nplans are prepared by GPs. The guidelines also enjoin upon States t o constitute a High Level \nMonitoring Committee (HLMC) headed by the Chief Secretary for monitoring and concurrent \nevaluation of local bodies and to ensure that funds are utilized for the purpose recommended by the \nFFC. Ministry of Panchayati Raj (MoPR) has  constituted a Coordination Committee headed by \nSecretary, Panchayati Raj to inter -alia guide and support States and local bodies in the \nimplementation of FFC recommendations, monitor progress of expenditure of grants by local \nbodies and suggest remedial measures as per the MoF Guidelines. To facilitate better monitoring of \nrelease/ capturing sector-wise expenditure made by GPs from FFC grants, MoPR has develo ped an \nonline system (MIS)   https://ffconline.gov.in.  \n \n  Release and transfer of the grant is subject to audit by the Comptroller and Auditor \nGeneral of India (C&AG). Further, States are also encouraged to adopt PRIASoft, an accounting \nsoftware application developed by the Ministry, to facilitate tracking  fund flow  and monitoring  \nexpenditures by the Gram Panchayats under FFCgrants. \n \n(c)  No. \n(d)  Does not arise in view of (c) above. \n********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |713", "t": "Overbridge on NH Passing through Santhal Pargana in Jharkhand", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 713 \nANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2019 \n \nOVERBRIDGE ON NH PASSING THROUGH SANTHAL PARGANA IN JHARKHAND \n \n713. SHRI VIJAY KUMAR HANSDAK: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक़ परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the proposals for construction of over bridges on National Highways passing \nthrough Sahibganj, Pakur, Godda and Dumka district under Santhal  Pargana of Jharkhand \nare pending with the Union Government at present along with the number of over bridges \nproposed to be constructed thereunder; \n(b) the names of locations where over bridges are proposed to be constructed; and \n(c) the efforts made by the Government for early disposal of said pending Projects? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI MANSUKH L.MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to (c) A total of 07 nos. bridges over railway lines have been envisa ged to be constructed at \nfollowing locations: \nSr.No. Location Chainage National Highway  \n1 Motijharna km 217.718 NH-80 \n2 Maharajpur km 219.020 NH-80 \n3 Rajmahal km 239.88 NH-80 \n4 Ambadiha km 1.040 NH-133B \n5 Lalmatia km 32.650 NH-133 \n6 Lalmatia km 30.477 NH-133 \n7 Hansdiha km 94.970 NH-133 \n \nThe request for GAD approval of these structures have been uploaded on Railways \nportal.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |717", "t": "Review of Films", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 717 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nREVIEW OF FILMS \n \n717. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has received any proposals for review of films; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the nature of proposals; and  \n \n(c) the details of the considerations made by the Government on those proposals? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY \nOF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING  \n[COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)]: \n \n(a) to (c): Yes Madam, Government has been receiving  representations on the \nissue of certification in respect of various films.  \nSection 6 of the Cinematograph Act, 1952 provides for revisional power of the \nCentral Government, however, this has been struck down by the Hon’ble Supreme \nCourt of India in AIR 2000 SC 3678 with respect to any certification issue decided by \nthe CBFC or Film Certification Appellate Tribunal (FCAT). \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |718", "t": "Closure of Aakashvani National News Channel", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 718 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019) \n \nCLOSURE OF AAKASHVANI NATIONAL NEWS CHANNEL \n718. SHRI KONAKALLA NARAYANA RAO: \n SHRI GUTHA SUKENDER REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING  \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to close the Aakashvani National News \nChannel; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;  \n \n(c) whether the Federation of Journalists have requested the Government to \nreview its decision; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) the stand/ reaction of the Government thereto? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nINFORMATION AND BROADCASTING (COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.) \n \n(a) & (b):  AIR FM coverage has been increasing continuously with 24 hour \nbroadcasts and hourly news headlines and bulletins on FM Rainbow and FM \nGold. Hence National Channel which was 30 year old had become replaceable. \nListenership of National Channel had declined drastically and was almost \nnegligible due to other new offerings by AIR and other technical reasons. In the \nlight of the shrinking listenership and  focus on modernization, it was decided to \nclose the channel in 2018.  \n \n(c), (d) & (e): No request from Federation of Journalists has been received by this \nMinistry. \n***********  \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |719", "t": "DD-Arunprabha", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 719 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019) \n \n  DD – ARUNPRABHA \n \n719.  SHRI L.R. SHIVARAME GOWDA: \n  SHRIMATI ANJU BALA: \n  SHRI TEJ PRATAP SINGH YADAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the proposals for commissioning of Programmes for Doordarshan (DD) – \nArunprabha were invited on the DD Website/Newspapers in December 2016 and \nif so, the details thereof including proposals received for the same; \n \n(b) whether any screening committee was formed to scrutinize and short - listing of \nabove proposals and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Prasar Bharati has further constituted any Evaluation Committee to \nselect proposals for  commissioning category for DD -Arunprabha out of these \nproposals and if so, the details thereof along with  the list of selected proposals \nunder open competition; and \n \n(d) the details of proposals given for permission to shoot the programme in this \nregard; and \n \n(e) the details of proposals yet to be given for the said purpose and the time by \nwhich the permission letters are likely to be issued? \n \n \n                                                               ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nINFORMATION AND BROADCASTING (COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.) \n \n(a) & (b): Prasar Bharati has informed that proposals for commissioning of  programme \nfor DD -Arunprabha were invited through an advertisement placed on the website of \nDoordarshan and in the leading newspapers. 1104 proposals were received.  Six \nScreening Committees were constituted by Prasar Bharati under the Chairmanship of \noutside experts to scrutinize and shortlist proposals. \n \n(c), (d) & (e): Prasar Bharati has informed that Five Evaluation Committees (EC) were \nconstituted to select proposals and 59 proposals from the open category were selected \nby committees. The list of the selected proposals was uploaded on DD’s website. Link is \nas under:- \nhttp://doordarshan.gov.in/sites/default/files/DD%20Arunprabha-\n%20List%20of%20selected%20proposals.pdf \n \nPermission letters are issued after obtaining requisite approvals.  \n                                              *********************** \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |72", "t": "Drinking Water in Schools", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 72 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 4TH FEBRUARY, 2019 \n \nDrinking Water in Schools \n \n†72. SHRI MANSHANKAR NINAMA:  \nSHRI AJAY MISRA TENI: \nDR. RAMESH POKHRIYAL “NISHANK”: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the number of schools including Kendriya Vidy alayas and Navodaya Vidyalayas in the country \nare lacking safe drinking water and toilets for boys and girls, State/UT-wise; \n \n(b)  whether it is a fact that the clean drinking water and toilets are not available in some schools \nincluding Kendriya Vidyalaya, Air Force St ation, Arjungarh, New Delhi and Kendriya \nVidyalayas in Uttarakhand and Uttar Pradesh and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  the steps taken/being taken by th e Government in this regard; and  \n \n(d)  the time by which such facilities are likel y to be implemented in all the schools including \nKendriya Vidyalayas and Navodaya Vidyalayas ? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d) Unified District Information System for Education (U-DISE) collects data on various \nindicators on school education.  As per U-DISE data collected for academic year 2016-17, State/UT-\nwise number of schools lacking drinking water and to ilets for boys and girls across the country is at \nAnnexure. All Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) and Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas (JNVs) across the \ncountry have safe drinking water and clean toilets for boys and girls. \n \nSection 19 of the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009 \nprovides the norms and standards for a school as specified in the Schedule to the Act. The RTE norms \ninter alia provide for separate toilets for boys and girl s; and safe and adequate drinking water facility to \nall children, in school s.  The States and UTs, defined as th e 'appropriate Governments' under the Act, \nare implementing the provisions of the RTE Act, 2009 through their respective State RTE Rules.  \n \n The appropriate Governments have the re sponsibility and mandate  to provide school \ninfrastructure including dr inking water facility and toilets in schools in accordance with the norms \n\nprescribed in the Schedule to the RTE Act, 2009 and respective State RTE Rules.  States and UTs have \nbeen instructed to ensure that all the schools in respective St ate/UT, including those under the non-\nGovernment sector (private, aided schools etc.) shoul d have provision for separate toilets for boys and \ngirls; and safe and adequate drinking water facilities to all children, in accordance with the norms under \nRTE Act, 2009. All State Governme nts and UT Administrations have  been requested to promote \nhygiene practices in schools and take remedial measures to fill in gaps. \n \nThe Central Government has launched a new Inte grated Scheme for Scho ol Education-Samagra \nShiksha which subsumes the erstwhile Centrally S ponsored Schemes of Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA), \nRashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) and Teacher Education (TE) from 2018-19.  Samagra \nShiksha supports States and UTs for strengthening of existing government schools, and for creation and \naugmentation of infrastructure facilities including drinking water and toilets in schools based on the \ngaps determined from Unified District Inform ation System for Education (UDISE) and proposals \nreceived from respective State/UT. \n \nUnder erstwhile SSA till 2017-18 and Samagra Shik sha effective from 2018-19, construction of \n3.99 lakh boys toilets, 5.22 lakh separate girls toilets and 2.40 lakh drinking water facilities have been \nsanctioned for elementary education to States and UT s.  Out of which, Stat es and UTs have reported \ncompletion of construction of 3.77 lakh boys toilets,  5.10 lakh separate girl s toilets and 2.34 lakh \ndrinking water facilities till 30.09.2018.  Under er stwhile RMSA till 2017-18 and Samagra Shiksha \nfrom 2018-19, 20,403 toilet blocks and 11,892 drinking water facilities have been sanctioned for \nsecondary/senior secondary schools to  States and UTs. Out of whic h, States and UTs have reported \ncompletion of construction of 14,474 toilet blocks a nd provision of 10,059 drinki ng water facilities till \n30.09.2018. \n \nThe Ministry of Human Resource Development had undertaken Swachh Vi dyalaya initiative to \nprovide separate toilets for girls and boys in all Government schools within one year upto 15 th August, \n2015. Under the initiative, construction/re-constr uction of 4,17,796 toilets was completed in 2,61,400 \nGovernment elementary and secondary schools in one year till 15th August 2015. \n \nThe Department has instituted Swachh Vidyalaya Puraskar from 2016-17 at district, state and \nnational level as a next step to Swachh Vidyalaya initiative. This has institutionalized a Swachhata \nranking system for excellence in water, sanitation and hygiene in schoo"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |721", "t": "Greenfield Highways to connect Industrial Hubs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 721 \nANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2019 \n \nGREENFIELD HIGHWAYS TO CONNECT INDUSTRIAL HUBS \n \n721. SHRI VENKATESH BABU T.G.: \nSHRI C. MAHENDRAN: \n \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक़ परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has projects specifically aimed at bridging critical infrastructure \ngaps through thedevelopment of economic corridors, so -called inter-corridors and feeder \nroutes, border and international connectivity roads, coastal and port connectivity roads and \nGreenfield expressways across the country and if so, the details thereof, State-wise; \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the estimated cost of the project; and \n(c) whether such projects are much delayed and the time by which the above new highways \nare likely to be constructed? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI MANSUKH L.MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to (c) The Bharatmala Pariyojana comprises of about 65,000 km of Highways / Roads. The \ntentative State -wise details are provided at Annexure. Out of total length under \nBharatmala Pariyojana, 24,800 km Highways (i.e. economic corridors, inter corridors, \nfeeder routes, border roads etc.) along with 10,000 km residual NHDP stretches have \nenvisaged for construction under Phase -I of Bharatmala Pariyojana over a period of 5 \nyears i.e. from 2017 -18 to 2021 -22 for which Rs. 5,35,000 crores have been \nearmarked. There is no delay. \n  \n\nAnnexure \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 721 FOR ANSWER ON 07-02-2019 ASKED BY SHRI VENKATESH BABU T.G. \nAND SHRI C. MA HENDRAN REGARDING GREENFIELD HIGHWAYS TO CONNECT \nINDUSTRIAL HUBS. \nState-wise details of roads and highways to be constructed under Bharatmala Pariyojana. \nSr. no. State Approximate length (km) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 3,600 \n2 Assam 2,190 \n3 Bihar 1,670 \n4 Chhattisgarh 950 \n5 Delhi 20 \n6 Goa 200 \n7 Gujarat 4,200 \n8 Haryana 2,310 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 200 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 430 \n11 Jharkhand 960 \n12 Karnataka 4,220 \n13 Kerala 1,240 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 5,850 \n15 Maharashtra 8,340 \n16 Manipur 920 \n17 Meghalaya 550 \n18 Mizoram 1,080 \n19 Nagaland 450 \n20 Odisha 3,110 \n21 Punjab 2,130 \n22 Rajasthan 5,500 \n23 Sikkim 160 \n24 Tamil Nadu 4,350 \n25 Telangana 1,840 \n26 Tripura 650 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 4,800 \n28 Uttarakhand 830 \n29 West Bengal 2,250 \n30 Total 65,000 \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |722", "t": "NHs in Karnataka", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 722 \nANSWERED ON  7th FEBRUARY, 2019 \n \nNHS  IN KARNATAKA \n \n722.  KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n    सड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the total length of NHs increased from about 91287 km in 2014 to  about \n131326 km at present ; \n \n(b) whether the Government has received proposals from the Government of Karnataka \nfor declaration of State Highways (SHs) as new National Highways during the last four \nyears, if so, the details thereof, year-wise ; \n \n(c) the details of new NHs declared during the last four years and the current year in \nKarnataka ; \n \n(d)    the length of State Highways approved “IN -PRINCIPLE” for their declaration as new \nNHs subject to outcome of their DPRs in Karnataka during the last four years,  year -\nwise ; and \n \n(e)     the status of construction of SHs as new NHs : Karkala -Moodbidri-BC Road; BC Road-\nKateel-Mulki Road, Sringeri - Balehonnur-Nadipur and Kundapur - Agumbe-Sringeri  \nRoad ? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n \n \n(a) Yes Madam.   \n\n \n \n(b) & (c)  37 Proposals for declaration of about 6800 Km of State Highways as new NHs \nhave been received from the State Government  of Karnat aka during the last four \nyears.  The year wise details of NHs declared during the last four years and current \nyear for the State of Karnataka are as follows: \n \nYear Length of NHs  notified  \n(in Km.) \n2014-15 138 \n2015-16 70 \n2016-17 259 \n2017-18 889 \n2018-19 (Till Dec.2018) 132 \n \n(d) The year-wise length of State Roads  approved “IN -PRINCIPLE” for their \ndeclaration as new NHs subject to outcome of their DPRs in Karnataka during the last \nfour years are as follows:  \n \nYear Length of In - Principal \napproved NHs  (in Km.) \n2014-15 Nil \n2015-16 3522 \n2016-17 2999 \n2017-18 88 \n \n  (e)    All four roads i.e Karkala-Moodbidri-BC Road; BC Road -Kateel-Mulki Road, \nSringeri- Balehonnur-Nadipur and Kundapur- Agumbe-Sringeri  Road are at DPR stage \nand their declaration  as new NHs depend  upon final out come of DPR , inter-se priority \nand availability of funds.  \n \n \n******* \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |723", "t": "Regulations for Axle Load", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 723 \nANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2019 \n \nREGULATIONS FOR AXLE LOAD \n \n723.  DR. K. GOPAL: \n ADV. M.UDHAYAKUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has decided to increase the axle loads after taking into consideration the \nqualitative and quantitative changes in National Highways as well as reforms in heavy automobiles \nand if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the automobile technology and road construction quality has improved considerably; \n(c) whether there is a need to harmonise axle loads with international standards and the Government \nhas made changes to regulations governing it; \n(d) whether the requirement for annual fitness certificates to trucks have been done away with and \nthese certificates nowneed to be only renewed every two years; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to (c) Yes Madam . The Government has increased the axle loads by this Ministry’s \nNotifications S.O. 3467(E) dated 16.07.2018 and S.O. 3881(E) dated 06.08.2018 , after taking \nstakeholders and concessionaires into the consideration. With the advancement and improvements in \ntruck manufacturing technology, and the National Highways designed to carry and support much \nheavier loads, the Government has considered for enhancing GVW norms. \n \nThe notification has been issued with a view to harmonise with International  standards where \nthe permissible axle load is 11.5 T for single driven and 10  T for single non-driven axle, irrespective \nof the number of tyres.  \n \n(d) & (e) The Government has increased the requirement of periodic fitness certificate to two \nyears for vehicles up to eight years old and each year for vehicles older than eight years by  this \nMinistry’s Notification S.O. 1081(E) dated 02.11.2018. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |724", "t": "Status of PMAY in Andhra Pradesh", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 724 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \n STATUS OF PMAY IN ANDHRA PRADESH  \n \n724.  SHRI M. MURALI MOHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the quantum of funds released to the Government  of Andhra Pradesh under the Pradhan \nMantri Awas Yojana (PMAY) during the last three years;  \n(b) the names of districts in the State covered und er the said Yojana; and  \n(c) the number of houses constructed/ likely to be constructed in East and West Godavari \ndistrict of Andhra Pradesh under the said Yojana?  \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV) \n \n(a) :   Funds released to the State of Andhra Pradesh under  Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana-\nGramin (PMAY-G) during 2015-16 to 2017-18 is Rs.850 .17 Crores and under Pradhan Mantri \nAwaas Yojana-Urban (PMAY-U), Central Assistance rel eased is Rs. 3280.66 crores during the \nlast three years. \n  \n(b): All the districts of the State of Andhra Prade sh are covered under PMAY-G while names \nof districts covered under PMAY(U) is at Annexure-I. \n \n(c): As per AwaasSoft report, as on 04.02.2019, for  PMAY-G and information provided by \nthe Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs  for PMAY(U), details of houses constructed/likely to \nbe constructed in East and West Godavari districts is given below: \n (Units in No.s) \nDistricts PMAY-G PMAY-U \nHouses \nConstructed \nHouses likely to \nbe constructed \nHouses \nCompleted \nHouses likely \nto be \nconstructed \nEast Godavari 6,408 18,467 23,100 21,618 \nWest Godavari 4,984 9,244 14,893 38,041 \n \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure referred to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No - 724 to be answered on  \n07.02.2019 \n \nName of Districts in the State of Andhra Pradesh covered under PMAY(U) \n \n \n \nSr. No. \n \n \nDistricts name  \n \n1 \n \nAnantapur \n \n2 \n \nChittoor \n \n3 \n \nEast Godavari \n \n4 \n \nGuntur \n \n5 \n \nKadapa \n \n6 \n \nKrishna \n \n7 \n \nKurnool \n \n8 \n \nPrakasam \n \n9 \nSri Potti Sriramulu \nNe llore \n \n10  \n \nSrikakulam \n \n11  \n \nVisakhapatanam \n \n12  \n \nVizianagaram \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |726", "t": "Drinking Water Facilities in Schools", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.726 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nDrinking Water Facilities in Schools \n \n†726. SHRI GOPAL SHETTY: \n \nWill the Minister of DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the policy of the Government to provide drinkin g water facilities in the schools \nlocated in rural areas;  \n(b) whether any targets have been fixed during the last Five Year plan to provide \ndrinking water facilities in these schools; \n (c) if so, the details thereof and the State-wise achievements made in this regard during \nthe said period; \n (d) whether the fixed targets could not be achieved in many States; \n (e) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and  \n(f) the steps taken/proposed to be taken to achieve the targets? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n(SHRI RAMESH CHANDAPPA JIGAJINAGI) \n \n(a) to (f) The Ministry of Drinking Water and Sanitatio n provides technical and \nfinancial assistance to States under the National R ural Drinking Water Programme \n(NRDWP) for supplementing their efforts in providin g drinking water supply in \nrural areas including schools. The assistance under  the programme includes \nprovision of drinking water supply in schools which  were constructed prior to 2007. \nFor schools constructed post 2007, facility specifi c to school is funded through \nMinistry of Human Resource Development (MoHRD). Sta tes have been advised to \nprovide facilities in the vicinity of school premis es while planning / executing \nNRDWP water supply schemes. \n \nAs informed by Ministry of Human Resource Developme nt, Department of School \nEducation and Literacy, state-wise details of perce ntage of drinking water facilities \nand functional drinking water facilities in Governm ent Schools is given at \nAnnexure-I. \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in part (a) to (f) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 726 for \nanswer on 07/02/2019. \nState-wise details of Percentage of Drinking Water facilities and functional Drinking \nWater facilities in Government schools \nS. No. State/UTs Schools with  \ndrinking water  \nfacilities \nSchool with functional  \ndrinking water  \nfacilities \n1.  ANDAMAN & NICOBAR ISLANDS  100  95.93  \n2.  ANDHRA PRADESH 93.24  83.64  \n3.  ARUNACHAL PRADESH 79.5  65.45  \n4.  ASSAM 92.79  84.67  \n5.  BIHAR 94.52  89.86  \n6.  CHANDIGARH 100  100.00  \n7.  CHHATTISGARH 99.22  89.59  \n8.  DADRA & NAGAR HAVELI 100  98.01  \n9.  DAMAN & DIU 100  97.48  \n10 . DELHI 100  100.00  \n11. GOA 100  96.33  \n12 . GUJARAT  99.94  100.00  \n13. HARYANA 99.84  97.66  \n14. HIMACHAL PRADESH 100  96.72  \n15 . JAMMU & KASHMIR 91.25  81.40  \n16 . JHARKHAND 96.22  90.73  \n17 . KARNATAKA 99.62  98.14  \n18 . KERALA 99.35  95.91  \n19. LAKSHADWEEP 100  100.00  \n20 . MADHYA PRADESH 95.56  82.94  \n21 . MAHARASHTRA 99.48  94.79  \n22. MANIPUR 96.63  66.74  \n23 . MEGHALAYA 62.34  51.32  \n24. MIZORAM 93.53  79.48  \n25 . NAGALAND 79.92  64.92  \n26 . ODISHA 99.79  85.81  \n27 . PUDUCHERRY  100  100.00  \n28 . PUNJAB  99.99  99.99  \n29. RAJASTHAN 96.53  85.32  \n30. SIKKIM 97.6  84.41  \n31 . TAMIL NADU 100  97.85  \n32 . TELANGANA 98.96  73.28  \n33 . TRIPURA  89.68  77.85  \n34 . UTTAR PRADESH 98.11  87.90  \n35 . UTTARAKHAND  95.21  81.33  \n36 . WEST BENGAL 98.87  90.77  \n All State  96.96  88.52   \nSource: UDISE 2016-17 (Provisional) \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |727", "t": "Seaplane Service on Ganga River", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO,727\nTO BE ANSWERD ON 7IH FEBRUARY, 2019\nSEAPLANE SERVICE ON GANGA RIVER\n727. SHRI A. ARUNMOZHITHEVAN:\nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state:\n+d cftdBi ri*\n(a) whether the Government proposes to start seaplane and helicc,pter services\non 1680 km. length of the Ganga River for passenger transport;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(C) whether the Government has any proposal to start Such servicr:s from other\nareas also; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING\n(sHRl PON. RADHAKRISHNAN)\n(a) & (b) There is no proposal so far to start seaplane and helicopter operations on\n1680 km. length of Ganga river for passenger transport,\n(c) & (d) Under Regional connectivity scheme (RCS) UDAN version 3.0 of Minrstry of\nCivil Aviation, the routes connecting the following six water aerodrome sitr-'s have been\nawarded to start such services:-\n(i) Guwahati River Front\n(ii) Nagarjuna Sagar\n( iii) Sabarmati River Front\n(iv) Shatruniay Dam\n(v) Statue of Unity (Sardar Sarovar Dam)\n(vi) UmrangsoReservoir"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |728", "t": "Gas based Power Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.728 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nGAS BASED POWER PLANTS \n \n728.  SHRI B.V. NAIK: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware  that several gas based power plants \nhave been stranded; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the  reasons therefor, along with the action \ntaken thereto; \n \n(c)  whether any subsidies and tax  concessions are being offered to those  \nplants and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d)  whether the Government is now  planning to revive 25 GW stranded gas \nbased capacity to push consumption of the fuel  and transform the nation into a \ngas based economy; and \n \n(e)  the time by when a decision in the matter will be taken? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER  \nAND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n( SHRI  R. K. SINGH ) \n \n(a) & (b) :   A total of 14305 MW gas based power generation capacity is \nstranded in the country due to non -availability of domestic gas. The details of \nthese gas-based power plants are furnished at Annexure.  \n \n(c) :   There is no Customs duty on import of Liquefied Nat ural Gas (LNG) \nand Natural Gas (NG), if it is used for generation of electrical energy by a \ngenerating company as defined in section 2(28) of the Electricity Act, 2003 (36 of \n2003) to supply electrical energy or to engage in the business of supplying \nelectrical energy to the grid.  \n \n(d) & (e) :  Gas based power plants are free to import the Liquefied Natural \nGas (LNG), generate power and sell it to the consumers. Ministry of Petroleum \nand Natural Gas (MoP&NG ) have stated that domestic gas is being supplied to \nthe power sector as per current production level and domestic natural gas \nsupply to power sector can improve only in case production levels increase in \nthe future and as per prevailing MoP&NG’ s guidelines. \n*************** \n \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.  728 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 07.02.2019.  \n*************** \n  \nSTRANDED GAS BASED CAPACITY (ALL INDIA) \nSl. \nNo Name of Project Sector Developer  \nInstalled \nCapacity \n(MW) \nState \n1 GAUTAMI CCPP P GVK Gautami Power Ltd 464 ANDHRA PRADESH \n2 GMR - KAKINADA  \n(Tanirvavi) P GMR Energy 220 ANDHRA PRADESH \n3 JEGURUPADU CCPP  P GVK Indutries Ltd 220.5 ANDHRA PRADESH \n4 KONASEEMA CCPP P Konaseema Power 445 ANDHRA PRADESH \n5 KONDAPALLI  EXTN CCPP \n. P Lanco Power 366 ANDHRA PRADESH \n6 VEMAGIRI CCPP P GMR Energy 370 ANDHRA PRADESH \n7 SRIBA INDUSTRIES P PCIL Power & Holdings \nLimited 30 ANDHRA PRADESH \n8 RVK ENERGY P RVK Energy 28 ANDHRA PRADESH \n9 SILK ROAD SUGAR P SILK ROAD SUGAR 35 ANDHRA PRADESH \n10 LVS POWER P LVS Power 55 ANDHRA PRADESH \n11 GMR VEMAGIRI EXP P GMR Energy 768 ANDHRA PRADESH \n12 KONDAPALLI EXP ST-III P Lanco Power 742 ANDHRA PRADESH \n13 SAMALKOT EXP P Reliance Infra 2400 ANDHRA PRADESH \n14 CCGT BY PANDURANGA P Panduranga Energy 116 ANDHRA PRADESH \n15 PRAGATI CCGT-III S Pragati Power \nCorporation Ltd 750 DELHI \n16 RITHALA CCPP  P NDPL 108 DELHI \n17 DHUVARAN  CCPP(GSECL) S Gujarat State Electricity \nCorporation Ltd 112 GUJARAT \n18 UTRAN CCPP(GSECL) S Gujarat State Electricity \nCorporation Ltd 374 GUJARAT \n19 PIPAVAV  CCPP S GSPC Pipavav Power \nCompany Ltd 702 GUJARAT \n20 DHUVARAN  CCPP S Gujarat State Electricity \nCorporation Ltd 376.3 GUJARAT \n21 HAZIRA CCPP EXT S Gujarat State Energy \nGeneration Ltd 351 GUJARAT \n22 VATWA CCPP*  P Torrent Power 100 GUJARAT \n23 ESSAR CCPP  P Essar Power 300 GUJARAT \n24 UNOSUGEN CCPP P Torrent Power 382.5 GUJARAT \n25 DGEN Mega CCPP P Torrent Power 1200 GUJARAT \n26 RATNAGIRI (RGPPL-\nDHABHOL) C NTPC 1967 MAHARASHTRA \n27 CCGT BY PIONEER GAS \nPOWER LTD P Pioneer Gas Power Ltd 388 MAHARASHTRA \n28 GAS ENGINE BY ASTHA  P Astha Power 35 TELENGANA \n29 KASHIPUR SRAVANTHI ST-\nI&II P Sravanthi Energy 450 UTTARKHAND \n30 BETA INFRATECH CCGT P Beta Infratech 225 UTTARKHAND \n31 GAMA INFRAPROP CCGT P Gama Infraprop  225 UTTARKHAND \n  TOTAL     14305   \nC: Central Sector; S: State Sector; P: Private Sector; \n* Vatwa CCPP was retired in 2015-16 \n \n*************** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |729", "t": "Average Consumption of Coal", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.729 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nAVERAGE CONSUMPTION OF COAL \n \n729.  SHRI M. CHANDRAKASI: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total number of coal based thermal power plants in the country \nas on date and their average per day consumption/ requirement of coal for \ntheir operations; \n \n(b)  the average coal stock availability for these plants in terms of coal \navailability for number of operative days at present ; and \n \n(c)  the steps being taken to increase coal and coal stock availability for \nthermal power plants? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER  \nAND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n( SHRI  R. K. SINGH ) \n \n(a) :   The total number of coal based thermal  power plants in the \ncountry as on 31.12.2018 is 175. Based on the generation target for the \nyear 2018-19, the daily average coal requirement of these power plants is \n1.79 Million Tonnes (MT)/day. During April -December, 2018, the average \nconsumption by these power plants was 1.72 MT/day. \n \n(b) :   As on 31st December, 2018, the total coal stock available with \nthese power plants was 21.66 MT. Based on their average consumption of \n1.72 MT/day, the average coal stock availability in terms of number of \ndays is about 13 days. \n...........2. \n \n \n\n- 2 - \n \n(c) :   Government ha ve taken the following steps to increase coal \nand coal stock availability for thermal power plants: \n \ni. The availability of coal is being regularly monitored closely to \nensure that generation of power plant is not affected due to \nshortage of coal.  \n \nii. Coal Blocks have been allotted to central/state power utilities to \nimprove domestic coal availability.  \n \niii. The Government has introduced a scheme SHAKTI (Scheme for \nHarnessing and Allocating Koyala (Coal) Transparently in India) -\n2017, to provide coal linkages to the power plants which do not \nhave any linkage.  \n \niv. The Government has introduced flexibility in utilization of domestic \ncoal amongst power generating stations to reduce the cost of power \ngeneration. The State/Central Gencos would have flexibility to utilize \ntheir coal in optimum and cost effective manner in their own power \nplants as well as by tra nsferring coal to other State/Central/IPPs \nPower plants for generation of cheaper power. This ensures \navailability of coal to efficient plants. \n \n \n*************** \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |73", "t": "Issues Faced by SEZ Developers", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.73 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nELECTRIFICATION OF VILLAGES \n \n*73.  SHRI MALLIKARJUN KHARGE: \n \nWill the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether an estimated 3.14 crore rural households are still \nwithout electricity and majority of such villages are in Bihar, Uttar \nPradesh, Assam, Jharkhand, Odisha, etc.; \n \n(b)  whether the Ministry has decided to electrify each household \nby April this year; and  \n \n(c)  if so, the details of roadmap prepared for execution of this \nproject and the details of expenditure to be incurred for this \npurpose? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER \nAND  \nNEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n( SHRI  R.K. SINGH ) \n  \n \n(a) to (c) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*********** \n  \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRE D TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c ) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 73 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA \nON 07.02.2019 REGARDING ELECTRIFICATION OF VILLAGES. \n********* \n \n(a) to (c) : As informed by the States, there are 21.30 crore \nhouseholds in the country; of these 21.29 crore households have \nbeen electr ified up to 27.01.2019.  Remaining 83,015 un -electrified \nhouseholds are in Chhattisgarh and Rajasthan which are aimed for \nelectrification by March, 2019. \n \nGovernment of India launched Pradhan Mantri Sahaj Bijli \nHar Ghar Yojana – “Saubhagya” with an allocation of Rs. 16,320 \ncrore in order to achieve universal household electrification by \nproviding electricity connections to all remaining un -electrified \nhouseholds in rural and all poor households in urban areas by March, \n2019. Under Saubhagya, Government of India gives funds to the \nStates to the extent of 60% (85% for special category States) as \ngrant and additional grant of 15% (5% for special category States)  if \nmilestones are met. \n \n \n************* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |730", "t": "Construction of Barhi- Koderma Section of NH-31", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 730 \nANSWERED ON 7TH FEBRUARY, 2019 \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF BARHI- KODERMA SECTION OF NH-31 \n \n730. DR. RAVINDRA KUMAR RAY: \n \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक़ परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Union Government has approved the construction of road for Barhi -Koderma \nsection of National Highway-31 in Jharkhand; and \n(b) if so, the time by which the work is likely to be completed and the estimated cost of this \nproject? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI MANSUKH L.MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) & (b) Yes Madam. The work has been awarded for an amount of Rs. 276.17 Crores. The \nscheduled completion period is two years from appointed date. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|17|UNSTARRED |731", "t": "Details regarding NRDWP", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 731 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2019 \n \nDetails regarding NRDWP \n \n731. KUMARI SUSHMITA DEV: \n \nWill the Minister of DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of funds allocated, funds released and funds utilised under the National \nRural Drinking Water Programme (NRDWP) from the yea r 2014 to 2018, State-wise \nand year-wise;  \n(b) the details of the percentage of the population  in the State of Assam that does not have \naccess to safe and portable drinking water from the year 2014 to 2018; and  \n(c) the details of the percentage of Government sch ools and AnganwadiCentres with \naccess to safe and portable drinking water under th e National Rural Drinking Water \nProgramme from the year 2014 to 2018, State-wise and year-wise ? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n(SHRI RAMESH CHANDAPPA JIGAJINAGI) \n \n \n(a) \nThe State-wise and year-wise details of funds alloc ated, released and utilised \nunder the National Rural Drinking Water Programme ( NRDWP) from the year \n2014 to 2018 are at Annexure-I. \n \n(b)   This Ministry assists the States for improving th e coverage of drinking water supply \nin rural areas only. As per the information provide d by State of Assam on the online \nIntegrated Management Information System (IMIS) of the Ministry, the details of \nthe rural population not having access to safe and portable drinking water from \nthe year 2014 to 2018 are as under:- \nFinancial Year % of rural population not having acc ess to \nsafe drinking water \n2014-15 11.17 \n2015-16 12.80 \n2016-17 14.03 \n2017-18 12.71 \n2018-19 (As on 05.02.2019) 12.44 \n \n\n(c) The Ministry of Drinking Water and Sanitation p rovides technical and financial \nassistance to States under the National Rural Drink ing Water Programme (NRDWP) \nfor supplementing their efforts in providing drinki ng water supply in rural areas \nincluding schools. The assistance under the program me includes provision of drinking \nwater supply in schools which were constructed prio r to 2007. For schools constructed \npost 2007, facility specific to school is funded th rough Ministry of HRD. States have \nbeen advised to provide facilities in the vicinity of school / \nAnganwadiCentrespremises while planning / executing  NRDWP water supply \nschemes. \nAs informed by Ministry of Human Resource Developme nt, Department of School \nEducation and Literacy, state-wise details of perce ntage of drinking water facilities \nand functional drinking water facilities in Governm ent Schools is given at Annexure-\nII. \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nAnnexure referred to in part (a ) of Lok Sabha unstarredQ.No. 731  for answer on 07.02.2019                                                                Annexure -I \n(Rs.in Crore) \nS.No. State \n2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 (as on 5.2. 2019) \nAllocation Release Expenditure Allocation Release E xpenditure Allocation Release Expenditure Allocatio n Release Expenditure Allocation \n(BE) Release Expenditure \n1 \nANDAMAN & \nNICOBAR 0.86 0.81 0.43 0.38 0.16 0 0.45 0.22 0 0.43 0 0 0.6 2 0.31 0 \n2 \nANDHRA \nPRADESH 372.43 369.65 418.96 156.69 170.05 190.6 181.02 204  157.38 159.51 233.09 267.03 197.56 185.85 69.43 \n3 \nARUNACHAL \nPRADESH 87.21 86.83 96.5 58.08 65.4 66.23 100.89 92.84 95.7 1 77.51 63.08 62.93 96.95 90.89 50.52 \n4 ASSAM 501.1 585.32 586.83 268.26 277.58 216.19 401.43 348 .06 203.09 524.1 474.8 387.2 567.89 202.01 212.08 \n5 BIHAR 441.07 399.11 381.9 229.6 202.73 295.38 311.7 373.8 1 473.29 394.53 355.6 216.21 476.63 234.84 